export const GUIDE_KB = "### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Test Accounts (00-test-accounts.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nTest Accounts & Ground Rules\n\nBefore you start testing, set up the accounts listed below. Every guide in this series\nrefers to these same people by name, so you can follow along without getting lost about\n\"who am I right now?\"\n\nThe cast of testers\n\nThese are the sample accounts used throughout the guides. Real names and emails are up to\nyou — swap in anything you like — but keep the roles the same, because the app\nbehaves differently depending on which role is signed in.\n\nRole\nName we'll use\nEmail\nWhat they do in the app\n\nAdmin\nTia Johnson\ntia.admin@wbct-pilot.test\n\nRuns the back office. Sends invites, edits material pricing, creates zones and depots,\nbuilds pickup routes, approves gift card redemptions.\n\nResident\nResident Aguirre\nresident.aguirre@example.com\n\nThe primary homeowner tester. Signs up, orders bags, scans bags before pickup, checks\nher points, redeems a gift card.\n\nResident\nResident Goode\nresident.goode@example.com\n\nA second resident used when a test needs two households on the same route (for example,\nverifying the route builder orders stops correctly).\n\nOperator\nOperator Aguirre\noperator.aguirre@wbct-pilot.test\n\nThe driver. Signs in on a phone, starts the day's route, scans each bag he picks up,\ndelivers new bag sheets, closes out the route at the depot.\n\nDepot\nDepot Goode\ndepot.goode@wbct-pilot.test\n\nThe sorter. Signs in at the depot (tablet or laptop), opens each bag Aguirre dropped\noff, weighs the seven materials separately, flags contamination, and submits — which\nawards points to the resident and adds weight to the depot inventory.\n\nTip — use separate browsers.\nTesting flows across two roles at once (e.g. Tia invites Aguirre, Aguirre accepts) is much\neasier if you open each account in a different browser or a private/incognito window. You\ncan also use separate phones. Don't sign one account out just to sign another in — you'll\nlose your spot in the flow.\n\nHow to create these accounts\n\n•\nTia (admin) is created once, behind the scenes, by the dev team. You\nwon't sign her up through the public website — she comes preloaded. Ask the dev team for\nher password.\n\n•\nAguirre and Goode (residents) sign themselves up on the public website. You'll\nwalk through that in the Sign-up & sign-in guide.\n\n•\nAguirre (operator) cannot self-sign-up. Tia has to invite him by email\nfrom the admin dashboard first. You'll walk through that in the\nAdmin dashboard guide before you get to operator testing.\n\n•\nGoode (depot worker) also cannot self-sign-up. Tia invites her by email\nand also picks the depot she's assigned to — that link tells the app which\ninventory to update when Goode processes a bag. You'll do this when you get to the depot\nguide.\n\nWhy the asymmetry?\nHomeowners are the general public — anyone with a valid address in the pilot zone can sign\nup. Operators, depot staff, and managers are employees, so the system refuses to create\nthose accounts unless an admin has first sent an invite. This prevents random people from\ngiving themselves driver access.\n\nWhat to have ready before you test\n\n•\nA laptop or desktop browser for Tia's admin work.\n\n•\nA second device (phone or a second browser window) for Aguirre the operator — operator\nscreens are designed phone-first.\n\n•\nA tablet or laptop for Goode the depot worker — the process-bag screen has seven weight\nfields, so a real keyboard is easier than thumbs-on-glass.\n\n•\nA real address in the pilot zone for Aguirre and Goode. The zone is configured by the dev\nteam; if your address isn't recognized during sign-up, tell them and they'll widen the\nzone for testing.\n\n•\nA test credit card number is not needed. Bag orders use a placeholder\n\"pay\" button during the pilot — no real money moves.\n\nHow to report what you find\n\nEach guide has small empty checkboxes next to the things you\nshould verify. As you work through a guide, tick what passes. If something looks wrong, jot\na quick note — what you did, what you expected, what actually happened, and a screenshot if\npossible. Send the list back when you're done with the phase.\n\nDon't worry about breaking things.\nThis is a pilot build running against test data. You can create as many bags, orders, and\nroutes as you want; nothing is going to real customers or real mail. If something seems\nstuck, reload the page. If it stays stuck, that's a bug — write it down.\n\nWhat's stubbed in the pilot (not bugs)\n\nA handful of features intentionally don't fully fire during the pilot. Each role guide\nrepeats the subset that applies to it, but here's the full picture up front so you know\nwhat to expect:\n\n⚠️ Features that look real but are simulated\n\n• Paying for bag orders — checkout ends on a mock \"paid\" screen. No card is charged.\n\n• Gift card redemption — the request lands in an admin queue; an admin emails the card manually. There's no automatic gift-card API fulfillment.\n\n• \"Pay trash bill\" and \"Donate\" redemption options are disabled placeholders.\n\n• SMS notifications (operator en-route, bag-processed receipt) are logged to the server console, not sent by text.\n\n• Invitation emails are logged to the console; copy the invite link from the server log to simulate the employee opening it.\n\n• Compliance notices — the app generates PDFs; an admin prints, stuffs, mails, and clicks Mark as mailed by hand.\n\n• Weighing bags is manual — no Bluetooth/USB scale integration.\n\n• Driver-on-the-way is tap-triggered by the operator, not GPS-geofenced.\n\n← Back\nTesting roadmap\n\nNext · Step 3\nGlossary →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Test Accounts reference · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Accounts & Sign-in (accounts-and-signin.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nAccounts & Sign-in\n\nThe single guide for creating every kind of account and verifying sign-in works. Do this\nbefore any other phase — every later guide assumes these accounts exist.\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\n\n• Invite emails are not sent. When Tia creates an invite, copy the link from the admin UI and paste it into a private window (or forward to the invitee) — that replaces the emailed link.\n\n• Resident self-signup does not require email verification during the pilot.\n\n📋 Before you start\nDevice: 💻 Laptop for admin + signing up. 📱 Phone for operator once you've invited him.\n\nTwo paths — pick one:\n\n• Fast path (recommended): the dev team has already run npm run seed:pilot , which creates every account on the Test users card. Skip to § Verify the sign-in flows .\n\n• From scratch: if you want to walk through account creation yourself (e.g. to demo onboarding), follow § Manual account creation .\n\nHow each role is created\n\nThree paths, depending on the role. This is just reference — you'll actually execute the\nscenarios below.\n\nRole\nHow they're created\nWhy\n\nAdmin\nDev team (one-time) via npm run seed:pilot .\nNo admin exists yet to invite them.\n\nResident\nSelf-signup from the public homepage.\nResidents are general public — anyone with a valid address in the pilot zone can join.\n\nOperator\nAdmin sends an invite; employee accepts at a one-time link.\nEmployees — no self-signup, so random people can't give themselves driver access.\n\nDepot worker\nSame invite flow, plus admin picks the depot.\n\nDepot manager\nSame invite flow, plus admin picks the depot.\n\nVerify the sign-in flows\n\nWork through these five scenarios in order. If the pilot seed has already run, accounts\nare ready — you're just checking that each one logs in to the right area of the app.\n\nScenario 1 — Admin signs in\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia · tia.admin@wbct-pilot.test .\n\n• Open the homepage in your main browser.\n\n• Click Sign in (alongside Get started in the landing-page header).\n\n• Enter Tia's email and the password from the Test users card .\n\nLanding & login navigation NEW · Jun 9\n\nThe landing page now offers a Sign in link next to Get\nstarted . The login screen carries a back arrow and the\nWe Buy Clean Trash. wordmark, both of which link back to the homepage —\nso a visitor who clicked Sign in by mistake can get back without using the browser\nbutton.\n\nExpected: Tia is redirected straight to the admin dashboard (not the\nresident home). She sees KPI tiles and a left sidebar with Users, Zones, Pricing,\nRedemptions, Routes.\n\nVerify:\n\nLanding screen is the admin dashboard, not resident home\n\nAdmin sidebar is visible\n\nScenario 2 — Resident self-signup (email + password)\n\nResident In a private/incognito window.\n\nThis is the only scenario where you create a new resident on top of the seeded\nones — useful for confirming the welcome bonus fires for a brand-new account.\n\n• Open the public homepage in a fresh private/incognito window.\n\n• Click Sign up .\n\n• Enter a name, an email you control, a password, and a real street address in the pilot zone.\n\n• Submit.\n\nExpected: The new resident lands on the resident home screen with a\n10-point signup bonus (= $0.10) and a short 3-step onboarding card.\nA welcome email also goes out to the address they signed up with.\n\nVerify:\n\nName appears correctly in the top-right menu\n\nPoints balance shows 10\n\nAddress is saved (check the Profile tab)\n\nA welcome email arrives at the signup address\n\nScenario 3 — Resident sign-up with Google\n\nResident A different private window.\n\n• Open the homepage.\n\n• Click Sign up → Continue with Google .\n\n• Pick a Google account.\n\n• Fill in the remaining form fields (address, phone).\n\nExpected: Same as Scenario 2 — lands on the resident home screen with\n10 points. No password prompt.\n\nVerify:\n\nGoogle sign-in completes without asking for a password\n\nName (and photo, if the Google account has one) appear in the top-right menu\n\nScenario 4 — Operator cannot self-sign-up\n\nTester In a fresh private window, no one signed in.\n\n• Open the homepage.\n\n• Click Sign up .\n\n• Look at the form. There should be no role picker or \"I'm an operator\" option.\n\nExpected: No self-select for operator, depot, or admin. The only path\nto those roles is an invitation — tested in Scenario 5.\n\nVerify:\n\nNo role selector anywhere on sign-up\n\nScenario 5 — Admin invites an operator, operator accepts\n\nAdmin + Operator Two browsers (one for Tia, one private for the invitee).\n\nThis scenario creates a brand-new operator alongside the seeded one — useful to confirm\nthe invite flow end to end.\n\nAdmin side — send the invite\n\n• Signed in as Tia, open /admin/invites (sidebar → Users & Invites ).\n\n• Click Send invite .\n\n• Enter a throwaway email (one you control), pick role Operator .\n\n• Click Send .\n\n•\nThe invite appears in the list with status pending . Click it to copy the\ninvite link (email delivery is stubbed — you'll forward the link yourself).\n\nOperator side — accept the invite\n\n• Paste the link into a fresh private/incognito window.\n\n• Click Accept invitation .\n\n• Enter a new password, then re-enter it in the Confirm password field. The two must match, or submit is blocked.\n\n• Submit.\n\nConfirm-password on invite signup NEW · Jun 9\n\nInvite signup for every non-resident role (operator, depot worker, depot manager,\nadmin) now asks for a password and a confirm-password field. Deliberately type\ntwo different values — the form should refuse to submit until they match.\n\nExpected: The new operator lands on the operator home screen\n( Today's Route ), not the resident home. Opening the same invite link\na second time reports \"already used.\"\n\nVerify:\n\nThe invite appears in Tia's list with status pending\n\nNew operator lands on the operator screen, not resident\n\nSame link reused → \"already used\"\n\nFor depot-worker / depot-manager invites, a Depot dropdown appears (try it with a second invite)\n\nSigning out\n\nFrom any signed-in screen, open the top-right menu and pick Sign out . You\nshould return to the public homepage. If you reload and still see the logged-in view,\nthat's a bug.\n\nTip — one browser per role.\nTesting cross-role flows (admin invites → operator accepts → depot processes) is much\nsmoother with one browser or private window per role. Don't sign in and out of a single\nwindow — you'll lose your place in the flow.\n\nManual account creation (optional)\n\nOnly follow this section if you're onboarding a client who wants to see the signup\nexperience from scratch, or if the pilot seed hasn't been run. Otherwise skip it.\n\nThe first admin\n\nThe dev team creates the first admin via the pilot seed script. Ask for the email and\npassword — nothing self-serve here, because there's no admin to invite them.\n\nKeep the admin password somewhere safe.\nEvery invite in the pilot flows from the admin account. Lose access and nobody else can\ncreate operators or depot workers until the dev team resets it.\n\nA new resident (self-signup)\n\nSame as Scenario 2 above. No invite required — just a real address in the pilot zone.\n\nA new employee (operator / depot / manager)\n\nSame two-step flow as Scenario 5. The Depot dropdown appears only for depot-worker and depot-manager roles.\n\nQuick troubleshooting\n\nWhat you see\nProbably because\nWhat to try\n\n\"This address is outside our service area\" on sign-up\nThe address's zip isn't in any pilot zone.\nAsk the dev team to add the zip to the zone list.\n\nGoogle sign-in popup closes with no result\nBrowser popup blocker or a third-party cookie setting.\nAllow popups for the site and retry.\n\nInvite link says \"invalid or expired\"\nAlready accepted, or past the 7-day window, or revoked.\nAsk Tia to send a fresh one.\n\nEmployee lands on the resident home after accepting\nBrowser session cached before the role claim applied.\nSign out, sign back in, refresh.\n\nNo Depot dropdown when inviting a depot worker\nNo depots exist yet.\nCreate one under Zones & Depots first.\n\nResident signs in and lands on the admin page (or vice versa)\nRole-routing bug.\nWrite it down with the exact email used and send to the dev team.\n\n← Previous · Step 3\nGlossary\n\nNext · Step 5\nAdmin — your main path →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Accounts & Sign-in · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Compost · Admin: Commercial accounts (compost-admin-commercial-accounts.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nCompost · Admin — Commercial accounts\n\nOnboarding compost-collection sites and provisioning their reusable bins. Replaces\nTia's Linktree + Google Sheet \"Directory\" tab. Run as Tia Johnson .\n\n⚠️ Where this fits\nPhase B of the compost integration. Sites onboarded here become the operator's compost\nroute in Compost · Operator route . City-format\ndiversion reports across these sites are now live — see\nCompost · Diversion reports (Phase C).\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• Tia can add a commercial site (business name, contact, address, schedule, materials).\n\n• The site picks a default bin size and a list of pickup days.\n\n• An affiliation tag (Compost Clubhouse vs City of Columbus) can be set free-form.\n\n• Tia can provision N bins for the site — each gets a BIN-NNNN QR code that the driver scans on-site.\n\n• Archiving a site preserves its bins and historical pickups.\n\nScenario 1 — Add a Compost Clubhouse site\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• From the side nav, open 🏢 Commercial sites . (Sits between Bag stickers and Pricing.)\n\n• Click + Add commercial site .\n\n• Fill out:\n\n• Business name: Comune\n\n• Contact name: Joe Galarraga\n\n• Contact phone / email: optional\n\n• Street / unit / city / state / ZIP\n\n• Zone: the zone you set up in the admin Zones page\n\n• Default bin: 32 Gallon\n\n• Pickups / week: 2\n\n• Bins on site: 3 — the declared count of bins at the\nlocation. Shows in the \"Bins on Site\" column of the diversion report. (Metadata only;\nit doesn't have to match the number of QR-coded bins you provision below.)\n\n• First month of data: optional YYYY-MM — the month this\nsite started collecting. Used as the site's report start so \"months active\" is counted\nfrom the right point. Tia's imported sites already have this set from her spreadsheet.\n\n• Affiliation tag: compost_clubhouse\n\n• Collection days: click Mon and Thu\n\n• Materials: Food scrap should be pre-selected (it's the only bin-fullness material seeded by Phase A). Leave it on.\n\n• Driver notes: \"Bin is in the alley behind the kitchen — gate code 4321.\"\n\n• Click Create site .\n\nExpected: The form closes and a card for Comune appears under\nActive sites . Bins counter shows 0 . The amber affiliation chip\nreads compost_clubhouse .\n\nScenario 2 — Provision 3 bins for the site\n\n• On the Comune card, click Provision bins (top-right of the bin section).\n\n• Pick size 32 Gallon , count 3 .\n\n• Click Add bins .\n\nExpected: The counter jumps from 0 to 3 bins\nprovisioned . Three new BIN-NNNN -coded BagDocs now exist in\nFirestore, each tagged reusable: true and commercialAccountId\npointing at Comune. (Print labels for these in a future Phase B.5 — for the pilot,\ntaping a printed number sticker on the bin is fine.)\n\nVerify:\n\nBins counter on the site card increments to 3\n\nTrying count: 100 is rejected (cap is 50/req)\n\nScenario 3 — Edit and archive\n\nArchiving must not destroy historical pickups or detach the bins.\n\n• On any active site card, click Archive .\n\n• Confirm.\n\nExpected: The site moves into the Archived section at the\nbottom. Its bins still exist in Firestore (verify by querying bags\nwhere commercialAccountId == <archived-id> ). Past binPickups\ndocs continue to reference the site by id.\n\nEdge cases worth poking\n\n• Zone required: The \"+ Add commercial site\" button is disabled until at least one zone exists.\n\n• Material picker: Only materials with measurementMode: 'bin_fullness' default-on. Cash recyclables (aluminum, etc.) can be selected too — it just means the operator could record those at this site if you ever want to.\n\n• Collection days mismatch: The form lets you pick fewer days than pickupsPerWeek says. That's intentional — drivers might double up on a single day.\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Compost · Operator route (compost-operator-route.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nCompost · Operator — Bin-fullness route\n\nHow a compost driver records a pickup at a commercial site. The driver opens the\ncompost route, taps the site, picks fullness per bin, submits. No scale needed — the\nserver runs the bin-weight lookup. Run as Operator Aguirre .\n\n⚠️ Prerequisites\n\n• An admin onboarded at least one commercial site in the operator's zone — see Compost · Admin: Commercial accounts .\n\n• That site has at least one bin provisioned (or the operator uses the manual-entry row).\n\n• Today is one of the site's collection days , or you're testing an off-day pickup (still allowed — sites just won't show the TODAY badge).\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• The compost-route banner appears on /operator when commercial sites exist in the operator's zone.\n\n• Today's sites surface above the rest with a TODAY badge.\n\n• The site stop screen shows one row per provisioned bin.\n\n• Picking a fullness bucket updates the per-row weight estimate live.\n\n• Total weight = sum across all rows with content.\n\n• Submitting writes a binPickups doc and increments commercial inventory.\n\nScenario 1 — Open the compost route\n\nOperator Signed in as Operator Aguirre, on a phone.\n\n• Land on /operator .\n\n• Look for the blue banner: 🥬 Compost route — N sites in zone .\n\n• Tap it. You land on /operator/compost .\n\nExpected: Today's sites bubble to the top with a TODAY badge in the\ntop-right corner of the card. The other sites (different collection days) sort\nalphabetically below. Each card shows zone, bin count, pickups/week, and the days\nthe site is collected.\n\nScenario 2 — Record a pickup at Comune (3 × 32-gal bins)\n\nComune was onboarded with three 32-gal bins. Today the driver finds the bins at\n¾, ½, and full.\n\n• Tap the Comune card. You land on /operator/compost/[id] .\n\n• Read the driver-notes amber banner if present (gate code, bin location).\n\n• Material defaults to Food scrap . Leave it.\n\n• Three rows are pre-rendered, one per provisioned bin. For each row tap the\nfullness bucket:\n\n• Bin 1 → 75% / Three-Quarter → reads ≈ 90 lbs\n\n• Bin 2 → 50% / Half → reads ≈ 60 lbs\n\n• Bin 3 → 100% / Full → reads ≈ 130 lbs\n\n• Watch the blue Total weight panel at the bottom — it should\nupdate live to 280 lbs across 3 of 3 bins with content .\n\n• Leave contamination on None .\n\n• Add a note like \"Hot day, lid was open\" (optional).\n\n• Tap the green ✓ Record pickup CTA.\n\nExpected: You bounce back to /operator/compost . The\ncard for Comune still shows TODAY (you might pick up again later). In Firestore:\na new binPickups doc with totalWeightLbs: 280 ; the\ninventory doc commercial_<zoneId>_food_scrap incremented by 280.\n\nVerify:\n\nTotal updates instantly when fullness changes\n\nSubmit is disabled when no row has fullness > 0\n\nA site with zero bins falls back to a single manual-entry row that lets you pick a size and fullness ad-hoc\n\nScenario 3 — 48-gallon weights\n\nAll bin sizes now use one linear formula —\nfullness × full-bin weight — matching the \"Alternative Weight\" column Tia's\nmonthly reports actually aggregate. Full-bin weights: 32-gal = 130 lbs, 48-gal = 195 lbs,\n64-gal = 260 lbs. So a 48-gal bin at 50% is exactly half of 195.\n\n• Provision a 48-gal bin for any site (Compost · Admin scenario 2).\n\n• On the operator stop screen, pick 50% on that row.\n\nExpected: The row reads ≈ 97.5 lbs (195 × 0.5). These are\ntreated as real values, not estimates — the linear model is the source of truth and\nreproduces Tia's three-year reporting history exactly, so the\nPhase C diversion reports do not footnote them.\n\nEdge cases worth poking\n\n• Material not accepted: Server rejects with material_not_accepted if the operator submits a material that isn't on the site's materialIds list.\n\n• Bin / size mismatch: If you somehow submit a bagId pointing at a 32-gal bin while the row says 64-gal, the server rejects with bin_size_mismatch .\n\n• Operator without a zone: If the operator's zoneId is unset, all active commercial sites show — useful for admin testing, not for production.\n\n• Photo capture: The form accepts an optional photo (wired but UI-deferred). Add it in a follow-up if drivers want to document overflow / contamination.\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Compost · Diversion reports, imported history & methodology (Tia FAQ) (compost-reports-and-data.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🏢 Commercial sites · 🚛 Operator route · 📖 Glossary\n\nCompost · Diversion reports, imported history & methodology\n\nThis guide explains the compost diversion reports , the three years of\nCompost Clubhouse history we imported, and — most importantly — why some\nnumbers in the app differ from Tia's master spreadsheet . It is written so the in-app\nAssistant can answer Tia's questions directly. Run as Tia Johnson (admin).\n\n⚠️ Where this fits\nPhase C of the compost integration. Builds on\nCommercial sites (Phase B) and the\noperator route flow . Compost is kept\ncompletely separate from the recycling/points side of the app — food scrap is\ndiversion-only and never touches points, transactions, or inventory.\n\nThe headline (what Tia should know first)\n\n• Her entire operation is already in the app. We imported everything from her\nmaster spreadsheet.\n\n• 22 sites — 12 Compost Clubhouse client sites + 10 City of Columbus drop-off\nsites (19 active, 3 archived).\n\n• Every pickup since July 2023 — about 1,528 collection records ,\nwith the weights, bin counts, and fullness exactly as she tracked them.\n\n• The monthly report numbers match her spreadsheet to the penny. Example: the\nCompost Clubhouse total for April 2026 comes out to 27,966.33 lbs in both the\nsheet and the app.\n\n• Compost Clubhouse and City of Columbus data stay separate from each other and from the rest of\nthe WBCT recycling business.\n\nThe diversion reports screen\n\nFound in the admin dashboard under the \"Compost · Tia\" section of the side nav,\nat Diversion reports ( /admin/compost/reports ). It is separated from\nthe recycling navigation by a labeled divider so the two businesses never get confused.\n\nHow to pull a report\n\n• Open Diversion reports from the Compost · Tia nav section.\n\n• Pick a Month from the dropdown (defaults to the latest month with data).\n\n• Pick an Affiliation : All sites , Compost Clubhouse , or\nCity of Columbus .\n\n• Read the KPI band and the two tables, then Export CSV or\nPrint / PDF to send to the City or a client.\n\nWhat it shows:\n\n• KPI band — Monthly and all-time totals in pounds , tons ,\nand \"cars off the road.\"\n\n• Monthly Summary table — per site: weight collected (lbs), average bins\ncollected, bins on site, and average fullness for the selected month.\n\n• All Time Summary table — per site: total lbs, total tons, and months\nactive.\n\n• Inactive sites are shown greyed out with blank monthly figures (a \"—\"),\nand are left out of the monthly total — the same way Tia blanks them before sharing a\nreport.\n\nWhat the imported history covers\n\n• All sites were imported from the master \"Directory\"/data tabs and tagged internally with\nimportSource: 'compost-workbook' so the import is reversible and re-runnable.\n\n• Sites live in a dedicated compost zone and depot, completely separate from recycling zones.\n\n• Three years of pickups means reports do not start from zero — they reflect her\nreal track record from day one.\n\n• A few orphan/junk rows from the sheet (2 sites with no data, 3 contact-only rows) were dropped\nduring import; everything with real pickup data was kept.\n\nWhy some numbers differ from the spreadsheet — and why that's intentional\n\nThis is the most important section for answering Tia's questions. Some numbers match exactly;\na couple are intentionally different. Here is each one.\n\n1. How a pickup's weight is calculated — matches her sheet ✅\n\nWe use the same method her reports already rely on: bins × fullness × full-bin weight .\nThe full-bin weights are her own verified values:\n\n• 32-gallon bin = 130 lbs when full\n\n• 48-gallon bin = 195 lbs when full\n\n• 64-gallon bin = 260 lbs when full\n\nFullness is recorded by the driver as 0 / 25 / 50 / 75 / 100%. The weight scales linearly — this\nreproduces the \"Alternative Weight\" column in her workbook exactly, so individual pickups and her\nmonthly totals come out the same.\n\n2. Monthly totals — match her sheet exactly ✅\n\nThe monthly report (the one she sends clients each month) reproduces her spreadsheet exactly for\nactive sites. The monthly math is:\n\nMonthly total = (total reported lbs for the month ÷ number of pickup entries) × 4.35\n\nThe 4.35 is the average number of weeks in a month — the same weekly-average ×\nweeks-per-month approach her sheet uses. Example: Chapman's first month had 585 lbs over 2 entries\n→ weekly average 292.5 → monthly 292.5 × 4.35 = 1,272.375 lbs .\n\n3. \"Cars off the road\" — kept the same for now, but flagged ⚠️\n\nWe kept her existing cars-equivalent calculation ( pounds ÷ 1.17 ) so her reports stay\nconsistent with what clients have already received. However , this number is far\nlarger than the standard EPA/ReFED conversion would produce (the standard figure is roughly\n0.00053 cars per pound — about 1,900× smaller). We flagged this in the code and in\nevery report footnote, and we are waiting on Tia's decision: keep her figure for consistency, or\nswitch to the standard conversion going forward. Both are available via a single setting\n( USE_REFED_CARS ); today it is set to reproduce her formula.\n\n4. All-time (\"lifetime\") totals — these run lower in the app\n\nThis is the main difference. When we examined how her spreadsheet computes lifetime totals, we\nfound three pieces of manual editorial logic built into the sheet over the years that are\nnot in the raw collection data:\n\n• Estimated \"gap\" months — when a site had no pickup recorded in a month, the\nsheet carried the previous month's estimate forward, adding weight that wasn't actually\ncollected.\n\n• Seasonal resets — for schools (e.g. The Wellington School), the running total\nis manually reset each school year and summer months are blanked as \"Closed for Summer.\"\n\n• Discontinued sites keep counting — sites that left the route (e.g. Chapman's)\nkept accumulating estimated weight every month afterward.\n\nThe app instead reports only weight that was actually collected — no estimated\nfill-ins, no carry-forward, no resets. This is more defensible if the City or a client ever asks\n\"how did you get this number?\", but it means lifetime totals in the app run lower\nthan her sheet for any site that had gaps or seasonal closures.\n\nConcrete example — Florin Cafe lifetime total:\n\n• Her spreadsheet: 33,374 lbs\n\n• The app (measured only): 28,390 lbs\n\nBoth are \"right\" — they answer slightly different questions (\"estimated diversion\" vs \"measured\ndiversion\"). Every report in the app includes a short methodology note explaining this. If Tia\nprefers the app to preserve her historical reported numbers as a baseline, we can do that — it is\none of the open questions below.\n\n5. Inactive sites in the monthly total — matches what she shares ✅\n\nSites no longer on the route are greyed out and excluded from the monthly total, mirroring how she\nblanks them before sending a report. They still appear in the all-time table for the historical\nrecord.\n\nWhat's still to come (planned, not built yet)\n\n• Full route operations — a \"Start / End Route\" day; per-stop buttons for\nSwapped vs Loaded , Skipped (with a reason), and Damaged\n(with a photo); a running truck-weight tracker with a \"truck is full → deliver to Alum Creek or\nLondon?\" prompt and an auto-text to the facility; and a cart cleaning queue at the yard.\n\n• Quarterly contamination notes — a place to log the recommendations and\ncontamination observations she keeps per site, carried into reports.\n\n• Automated monthly emails — those currently go out manually (via Dominique);\nthe app can send each site its monthly weight + cumulative + impact automatically.\n\n• Access for Dominique and the City — a \"program manager\" login for Dominique\n(manage sites, pickups, reports) and a read-only login for the City of Columbus to view only\ntheir own diversion reports.\n\n• Compost voucher rewards — for when the Columbus facility is producing.\n\nOpen questions for Tia\n\n• \"Cars off the road\" figure. Keep her current figure for consistency with past\nreports, or switch to the standard EPA/ReFED conversion going forward?\n\n• Lifetime totals. Show measured-only totals (cleaner, more defensible), or\npreserve her historical spreadsheet totals as a baseline so nothing appears to \"drop\"?\n\n• Seasonal closures. Should sites have a \"paused\" status so school summer breaks\ndon't look like missed pickups?\n\n• Monthly report format. Can we get a recent example of the exact report\nDominique sends the City, so the app's export matches it precisely?\n\n• Route operations details. Are Alum Creek and London the only drop-off\ndestinations? Who's the contact (phone) at each for the automatic \"on my way\" text? Who marks\nbins cleaned — the yard or the driver next day? What are the 4–5 most common skip reasons?\n\n• Site contact info. Several sites are missing a contact email — fill those in\nso monthly emails can reach them?\n\n• Branding. On screens the City or clients see, use Compost Clubhouse branding,\n\"powered by WBCT,\" or both?\n\nQuick answers to likely Tia questions\n\n• \"Is all my data in there?\" Yes — 22 sites and ~1,528 pickups since July 2023,\nfor both Compost Clubhouse and City of Columbus.\n\n• \"Do my monthly numbers still match?\" Yes, exactly — to the penny for active\nsites (e.g. April 2026 Compost Clubhouse = 27,966.33 lbs).\n\n• \"Why is my lifetime total lower?\" The app counts only weight actually\ncollected; the sheet added estimated gap-month weight, seasonal resets, and kept discontinued\nsites counting. It's a \"measured vs estimated\" difference, and it can be switched if she\nprefers.\n\n• \"Why does 'cars off the road' look different / can it stay the same?\" It is\ncurrently kept identical to her sheet (lbs ÷ 1.17). We flagged that it's much larger than the\nstandard figure and are awaiting her choice.\n\n• \"How does the weight get calculated?\" Bins × fullness × full-bin weight\n(130 / 195 / 260 lbs for 32 / 48 / 64-gal), same as her sheet.\n\n• \"Can I send this to the City?\" Yes — Export CSV or Print / PDF from the\nDiversion reports screen, filtered to City of Columbus.\n\n• \"Will compost mix with the recycling rewards app?\" No — it's fully separated;\nfood scrap is diversion-only and never awards points or touches the recycling ledger.\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Client Demo Script (demo-guide.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nClient Demo Script\n\nA 30–40 minute narrative walk-through of every feature, in the order that tells the story\nbest. Follow it top-to-bottom and your client will see the whole system — resident,\noperator, depot, manager, admin — without any \"now let me jump over here\" whiplash.\n\nHow to use this doc.\nEach step has a click ( ▸ ) and, where useful, a short line you can\nread out ( Say: ). You're not reading it like a teleprompter — use it as a safety net\nso you don't forget to show a feature. Total run time is roughly 35 minutes at a comfortable\npace; skip the optional bits if you need to hit 20.\n\nBefore the demo — 5-minute prep\n\n•\nOpen four browser profiles or windows and sign into each:\n\n• Resident Resident Aguirre — in your primary window (the one you'll screen-share).\n\n• Operator Operator Aguirre — on your phone, or a second window sized like a phone.\n\n• Depot Depot Goode — in a third window.\n\n• Admin Tia Johnson — in a fourth window; this is the \"back office\" you'll flip to last.\n\n•\nAs admin, pre-seed so the demo has something to show:\n\n• Resident Aguirre has a 10-bag sheet issued and at least one scanned bag queued.\n\n• A pickup route for today exists with Aguirre on it, assigned to Operator Aguirre.\n\n• Prices in /admin/pricing look current (no zeros).\n\n• There's at least one pending redemption in the queue so you can show the admin fulfilling it.\n\n•\nZoom your browser to ~110% so the client can read everything.\n\n•\nClose extra tabs, silence notifications, and put your phone on Do Not Disturb.\n\n•\nHave the full testing guide open in a spare tab in case the client asks \"can I try it later?\" — you'll send them that link.\n\nIf something breaks mid-demo.\nDon't panic and don't debug live. Say \"Looks like a bug — I'll log it and show you the\nnext piece.\" and move on. The script is modular; each act stands on its own.\n\nOpening · 2 min\n\nThe pitch in 90 seconds\n\nBefore clicking anything, frame the product.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash turns a household's recycling into cash-equivalent rewards. Residents\nsort into our bags, we pick up on a set day, and points land in their account based on the\nweight and market value of what they turned in. Points redeem for gift cards. What I'm\nabout to show you is a working pilot — every screen is live, wired to a real database and\nreal pricing.\n\nZoom to your landing page ( / ) and scroll top-to-bottom so they see the home experience before we sign in.\n\nAct 1 · Resident onboarding · 5 min\n\nA homeowner signs up\n\nStay in the resident window. Walk them through the front door of the app — what a real\nresident would see on day one.\n\nSign-up & the pre-signup AI scan\n\nOpen an incognito window side-by-side. Go to /scan .\n\nBefore a resident even creates an account, they can scan an item with their phone camera\nand our AI tells them roughly what it's worth. This is the hook — it's the first \"wow\"\nmoment that gets someone to sign up.\n\nHold up a soda can, cardboard box, or plastic bottle. Capture it. Show the result — material, estimated weight, estimated reward.\n\nClick Sign up or go to /signup .\n\nResidents self-signup with any valid address inside our pilot zone. Employees — operators,\ndepot, managers, admins — cannot sign up; they have to be invited by an admin. I'll show\nyou the other side of that in a few minutes.\n\nEither walk through the form to show it, or sign in as Resident Aguirre (who already exists).\n\nWelcome & the onboarding walkthrough\n\nIf a brand-new account: land on /resident/welcome .\n\nNew residents get a quick walkthrough and a signup bonus — 10 points, the equivalent\nof ten cents — so their account isn't empty on day one.\n\nResident dashboard\n\nGo to /resident .\n\nThis is home base for a resident. Their points balance, next pickup date, how many bags\nthey have left, and quick links to order more bags or run the calculator.\n\nOrdering bags\n\nClick Order bags ( /resident/order-bags ).\n\nBags are sold in sheets of ten. They're compostable, labelled with a unique QR, and\narrive on the next scheduled pickup. In the pilot we stub the payment step — in\nproduction this is a real Stripe checkout.\n\nPlace a test order so the client sees it enqueue onto a route.\n\nThe points calculator\n\nOpen /resident/calculator .\n\nResidents who want to understand how they get paid can play with this: pick a material,\nenter a weight, see what it would earn. It reads the same live pricing the depot uses, so\nwhat you see here is exactly what you'd be awarded.\n\nEnter 2 lb of aluminum. Then flip Declared separated on — point out the multiplier.\n\nIf the resident pre-sorts into a single-material bag and declares it, they earn 2×. That's\nthe behaviour change we're designing for — separation at the source.\n\nThe in-app guide\n\nClick Guide ( /resident/guide ).\n\nEvery resident screen has a help link — the guide is context-aware. It covers how to bag,\nwhat counts as contamination, pickup day rules.\n\nAct 2 · Pickup day (resident side) · 2 min\n\nScanning a bag the night before pickup\n\nStill as the resident, open /resident/scan-bag .\n\nOn pickup eve, the resident scans each bag they're putting out. Scanning tells us two\nthings: which bags are going out, and whether it's a sorted single-material bag — which\nis what earns the 2× multiplier.\n\nScan a pre-printed QR (or type in the fallback number). Mark it as aluminum, separated .\n\nWhat just happened under the hood.\nThe bag is flagged as \"scanned.\" Tomorrow the operator's route will list it, and the depot\nworker will see the declared material when they open it — so if it really is single-\nmaterial and clean, they apply the 2× multiplier.\n\nAct 3 · Operator day · 6 min\n\nBehind the wheel of the pickup truck\n\nSwitch to the operator window. Sign in as Operator Aguirre if you aren't already.\n\nToday's route\n\nLand on /operator .\n\nThis is the driver's phone view. One button — Start the route — and then an ordered list\nof stops. The order is optimized with Google Maps Routes so the driver isn't thinking\nabout directions, just executing.\n\nTap Start route .\n\nDoing a stop\n\nTap the first stop to open /operator/stop/... .\n\nEach stop shows the resident's name, address, how many bags they scanned last night, and a\n\"Driver on the way\" button that sends them an SMS two stops before arrival.\n\nTap Driver on the way .\n\nIn the pilot that SMS is stubbed — we log it in a collection called smsLog so we can\nverify the content before wiring Twilio for real.\n\nScan the bags at the stop using the phone camera. Mark any missing bags. Hit Complete stop .\n\nDelivering new bag sheets\n\nIf any stop has a pending bag order, the app flags /operator/deliver .\n\nWhen a resident orders new bags, the driver's next route automatically carries them. The\ndriver scans the sticker sheet onto the house — that's the handoff that ties physical bags\nto the resident account.\n\nClosing out at the depot\n\nFinish the last stop, then hit End route → /operator/summary .\n\nEnd of day, the driver drops the collected bags at the depot. The summary screen confirms\nevery bag handed off — chain of custody is intact.\n\nAct 4 · Depot processing · 6 min\n\nWhere the bag becomes money\n\nSwitch to the depot window. Sign in as Depot Goode.\n\nIncoming bags\n\nGo to /depot/incoming .\n\nEvery bag the driver dropped off is sitting in this queue, grouped by resident. The depot\nworker opens one, pours it onto the sort table, and starts weighing.\n\nProcessing a bag — the moneyshot screen\n\nClick a bag to open /depot/process/... .\n\nSeven weight fields, one per material — aluminum, tin and steel, cardboard, paper, PET,\nHDPE, mixed plastic. Each weight multiplies by today's market price for that material,\ntimes the resident's revenue share, and the total lands in the resident's account the\ninstant this form submits.\n\nEnter a few weights. Point at the running total that updates live.\n\nFlip Contamination: Minor .\n\nContamination knocks points down by 30, 60, or 90 percent depending on severity. It's the\nstick that matches the separation 2× carrot — so people learn to sort properly.\n\nSubmit. Watch the success toast.\n\nWhat just happened under the hood.\nOne atomic write — the bagProcessing record, a new row in the resident's ledger, an\nupdated points balance, and the depot's inventory all change together. You can never have\n\"I got paid but the inventory is wrong\" or vice versa.\n\nDepot inventory\n\nGo to /depot/inventory .\n\nRunning tally of every pound of every material currently on site. When we sell a load to a\nprocessor, the depot worker zeros that material out and logs the sale price — that's how\nwe close the loop on actual realized revenue versus the price we paid residents against.\n\nAct 5 · Back to the resident · 3 min\n\n\"Hey, I just got paid.\"\n\nFlip to the resident window. Refresh /resident .\n\nThe points balance jumped by exactly what the depot just awarded — live, no batch job.\n\nRedeeming\n\nClick Rewards ( /resident/rewards ).\n\n1,000 points equals $10. We're launching with Amazon and Walmart gift cards. In the\npilot, these are admin-fulfilled — I'll show you that queue in a moment. \"Pay trash bill\"\nand \"Donate\" are live stubs on the screen so residents see the roadmap.\n\nRequest an Amazon $10 gift card.\n\nProfile\n\nOpen /resident/profile .\n\nAddress, pickup day, notification preferences, and lifetime stats — total weight\nrecycled, total points earned, contamination strikes.\n\nAct 6 · Manager reports · 3 min\n\nRunning the depot as a business\n\nOpen a fifth window or reuse the admin window and sign in as the depot manager (or stay as admin — manager views are a subset).\n\nGo to /manager .\n\nThis is for whoever runs a depot day-to-day. Throughput, contamination rates, bags\nprocessed per worker, operator on-time rates.\n\nClick Reports ( /manager/reports ).\n\nClick Depots ( /manager/depots ).\n\nIf we scale to multiple depots, the manager picks between them here. One login, all their\nsites.\n\nAct 7 · Admin back office · 10 min\n\nThe whole business on one dashboard\n\nSwitch to the admin window. Sign in as Tia Johnson.\n\nLand on /admin .\n\nThis is mission control. Live inventory by material across every depot, zone-by-zone\nperformance, contamination alerts, operator leaderboard. Everything else in the sidebar is\none of the switches behind this dashboard.\n\nZones\n\nClick Zones ( /admin/zones ).\n\nA zone is a polygon on the map plus a pickup day. New service area? Draw a polygon, set\nthe day, and residents with matching addresses can now sign up.\n\nPricing\n\nClick Pricing ( /admin/pricing ).\n\nSeven materials, each with a market price and the resident's revenue share. Commodity\nprices swing weekly, so the admin keeps these current. Every edit snapshots to a price\nhistory — so if a resident ever disputes how much they earned on a specific pickup, we\nknow exactly what the price was that day.\n\nOpen aluminum, bump the price a penny, save.\n\nUsers & invites\n\nClick Users ( /admin/users ).\n\nThen Invites ( /admin/invites ).\n\nHere's the asymmetric auth I mentioned. Residents self-signup; employees get invited. Tia\npicks a role — operator, depot worker, depot manager, or admin — adds an email, and the\nsystem sends a one-time invite link that encodes the role. No rogue drivers.\n\nSend an invite to a throwaway email to demonstrate.\n\nRoute builder\n\nClick Routes ( /admin/routes ).\n\nEvery morning Tia builds tomorrow's routes. Pick the zone, pick the date, and the app\npulls every stop that needs service — scheduled pickups, bag deliveries, the works — and\norders them with Google Maps. She picks an operator and hits publish. The driver sees it\non their phone when they sign in.\n\nOpen an existing route, show the map and the ordered stops.\n\nBag sheets & QR printing\n\nClick Bags ( /admin/bags ).\n\nEvery time a resident orders a sheet, ten QR stickers need to exist in the system. The\nadmin generates a printable PDF here, feeds it through a Rollo thermal printer, and the\nphysical stickers tie back to the same bag records the driver will scan. Every sticker has\nboth a QR and a printed number as a fallback if the camera can't read it.\n\nRedemptions queue\n\nClick Redemptions ( /admin/redemptions ).\n\nRemember the Amazon gift card the resident requested a few minutes ago? It's sitting here.\nTia buys the gift card, pastes the code in, and the resident gets it — either by email or\nin-app. In production this becomes an automatic API call to Amazon's gift-card service.\n\nFulfill the one Resident Aguirre just requested. Flip back to the resident window and show it arrived.\n\nCompliance\n\nClick Compliance ( /admin/compliance ).\n\nIf a resident racks up repeated severe-contamination strikes, the system generates a\nmailed notice — certified-letter style. The app produces the PDF, Tia prints it and drops\nit in the mail, then clicks \"marked as mailed\" so the strike clock resets. That's the\ncompliance loop: it's strict, but it's also fair and documented.\n\nClosing · 3 min\n\nWrap, talking points, Q&A\n\nRecap in one breath\n\nWe walked a bag from a resident's curb through pickup, through the depot, to a gift card.\nEvery screen is live. Every number is calculated by code that's checked into a repo, not\nspreadsheets. This is ready to run a pilot with a real zone tomorrow.\n\nQuestions you'll probably get (and short answers)\n\n•\n\"How do you handle payments for real?\" — Stripe for bag orders, drop-in\nswap from the stub you saw. Gift cards auto-fulfilled through Amazon's Incentives API.\n\n•\n\"What if the camera can't read the QR?\" — Every sticker has a printed\nfallback number. The operator app has a manual-entry field for exactly that.\n\n•\n\"Can we scale to multiple depots?\" — Data model already has the fields.\nToday's UI is single-zone for the pilot; multi-zone is a UI change, not a rewrite.\n\n•\n\"What happens if a driver calls out sick?\" — Admin reassigns the route\nin the route builder with one dropdown change.\n\n•\n\"How do you prevent people from gaming the 2× multiplier?\" — The depot\nworker sees the declared material before they open the bag. If it's not what the\nresident said it was, they flag contamination and the multiplier doesn't apply.\n\n•\n\"Where does the data live?\" — Firestore (Google Cloud), backed up,\naccess-controlled at the row level. No resident can read another resident's data.\n\n•\n\"What's left to build?\" — Real Stripe, real Twilio, real gift-card\nAPI, a few polish passes. Core operations run today.\n\nLeave-behind\n\nSend them the full testing guide so they can poke at it after\nthe meeting, and a short summary email with:\n\n• A link to the deployed app and a test account.\n\n• Dates: when a pilot could start, how long it runs, what \"success\" looks like.\n\n• Any asks — partner zone, truck, depot space, budget.\n\nAfter the demo.\nNote what landed, what got a blank stare, and which feature they asked two follow-up\nquestions about — that's the one they actually care about, and it's where the next\niteration goes.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Client demo script · Generated for a live walk-through · Keep on\nlaptop during the demo, don't print it out.\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Glossary (glossary.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users\n\nGlossary\n\nEvery term the guides use that isn't obvious from context. If you hit a word you don't\nknow, check here first — chances are it has a very specific meaning in the pilot.\n\nJump to:\nApp terms\nRecycling terms\nRoles\nTech terms\n\nApp terms\n\nBag\n\nA single recycling bag with a unique QR sticker. The QR carries the bag's ID (printed as\nBAG-4237-03 , read as sheet 4237, bag 3 of 10). A bag is what a resident fills\nand a depot worker weighs.\n\nBag order\n\nA resident's request for more physical bags, placed from the Order more bags\nscreen. One order buys one or more sticker-sheet equivalents (10 bags each) and gets\nenqueued onto the next open route for the resident's zone so an operator delivers them.\n\nBag processing\n\nThe act of a depot worker weighing the 7 materials inside a bag, setting a contamination\nlevel, and submitting. One submission = one bagProcessing record and one\npoints-ledger entry for the resident.\n\nSticker sheet\n\nA printed page (2.25″×1.25″ labels on Rollo thermal stock) of 10 QR stickers that all\nshare a 4-digit sheet number. Generated by admin before operators can deliver bags.\nCreating the sheet atomically creates one stickerSheets doc + 10 bag records.\n\nRoute\n\nAn ordered list of stops an operator drives in one day. Contains pickups (scanned bags\nfrom residents) and deliveries (sticker sheets for bag orders). Tia builds routes the day\nbefore; the Google Maps Routes API optimizes stop order.\n\nPickup\n\nA completed visit where an operator scanned a resident's filled bag, optionally took a\ndoorstep photo, and marked the stop done. A pickup is different from \"delivery\" (dropping\noff new stickers) — one route can have both.\n\nPickup day(s)\n\nThe day or days of the week a zone is serviced. A zone can have multiple pickup\ndays per week , not just one. At signup the app matches a resident by ZIP and shows\nthem their assigned pickup day(s).\n\nYellow Sheet\n\nThe table of per-material market prices (dollars per pound) that admins maintain in\n/admin/pricing . Named after the industry term — a real recycling depot's\nweekly price list is traditionally printed on yellow paper. Changing a yellow-sheet value\nimmediately affects how many points residents earn from new bags.\n\nZone\n\nA geographic service area (one or more zip codes). Every resident is tied to one zone;\nevery depot serves one or more zones. The pilot uses a single zone (\"Kirk pilot\")\ncovering 80301.\n\nDepot\n\nA physical sorting facility where delivered routes are processed. Each depot has its own\ninventory (7 materials). Depot workers are linked to a specific depot at invite time so\ntheir processed bags update the right inventory.\n\nSignup bonus\n\nA one-time 10-point credit every new resident gets on signup.\nAppears as a signup_bonus transaction in the ledger. 1,000 pts = $10, so\nthe bonus is worth $0.10.\n\nRecycling terms\n\nCommingled\n\n\"All mixed together.\" The default bag type — the resident puts all their recyclables in\none bag without sorting. Gets the standard points multiplier (1×).\n\nSeparated\n\nThe opposite of commingled — the resident declares that their bag contains only one kind\nof material, or that they've pre-sorted it. Earns a 2× points multiplier\nbecause pre-sorted material is worth more to a depot.\n\nContamination\n\nNon-recyclable junk in a recyclable bag (food waste, plastic film in a paper bag, etc.).\nThe depot worker sets a severity level when processing; the severity reduces the points\nawarded.\n\nContamination severity\n\nOne of four levels set at process time:\n\n• None — clean bag. No penalty.\n\n• Minor — 30% penalty.\n\n• Major — 60% penalty.\n\n• Severe — 90% penalty (resident still gets credit, just very little).\n\nThe 7 materials\n\nEvery bag is weighed across these seven categories, each with its own market price:\naluminum, tin/steel, cardboard, paper, PET (clear bottles), HDPE (milk-jug-style), and\nmixed plastic (everything else).\n\nDiversion-only material\n\nA material that logs weight for diversion reporting but pays 0 points —\ne.g. commingled mixed loads or food scrap / compost. The weight still counts toward how\nmuch was kept out of the landfill; it just doesn't earn the resident any points.\n\nCampaign multiplier\n\nA per-material bonus an admin can run for a limited time (e.g. ×2 on\naluminum ). It boosts that material's contribution before the separated\nand contamination math is applied.\n\nMill shipment\n\nWhen a depot sends processed material to a recycling mill. Subtracts from the depot's\ninventory. The depot manager records these in the /manager pages.\n\nRoles\n\nResident\n\nA homeowner with an address in a pilot zone. Self-signs up. Orders bags, scans them\nbefore pickup, earns points, redeems them. Played by Resident Aguirre and Resident Goode\nin the guides.\n\nOperator\n\nThe driver. Runs routes on a phone, scans bags at each stop, delivers new sticker sheets,\ncloses out at the depot. Invite-only (no self-signup). Played by Aguirre in the guides.\n\nDepot worker\n\nWorks at a sorting depot. Weighs bags, flags contamination, submits points. Invite-only,\nand the admin must pick which depot they're linked to at invite time. Played by Goode.\n\nDepot manager\n\nRuns a depot. Sees inventory, records mill shipments, oversees depot workers. Invite-only\nand depot-linked. Covered in Phase 8 (not yet built).\n\nAdmin\n\nThe back-office role. Sends invites, edits pricing, creates zones/depots, builds routes,\nfulfills gift-card redemptions manually, prints compliance notices. Invite-only. Played\nby Tia.\n\nTech terms (rarely need these)\n\nCustom claims\n\nA Firebase Auth feature that tags a user account with extra info (e.g. role:\nadmin ). The app reads these claims to decide which pages you're allowed to see.\nInvisible to testers — they just determine where you land after sign-in.\n\nFirestore\n\nGoogle's cloud database. All app data lives there. Mentioned occasionally in expected\nblocks (\"a new bagProcessing doc appears in Firestore\") — mostly a concern\nfor developers.\n\nLedger / transactions\n\nThe immutable record of every points change. Signup bonus, pickup awards, redemptions —\neach writes a new row. A resident's current balance is always the running sum of their\nledger entries.\n\nStub\n\nA fake version of an external service used during the pilot (e.g. the SMS stub logs\nmessages to the server console instead of actually texting). See each guide's\n⚠️ What's stubbed box.\n\nIdempotent\n\nRunning the same operation twice produces the same end state as running it once. The\npilot seed script ( npm run seed:pilot ) is idempotent — re-running it doesn't\nduplicate test users.\n\n← Previous · Step 2\nTest accounts & ground rules\n\nNext · Step 4\nAccounts & sign-in →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Glossary · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash - How It Works (how-the-app-works.html)\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash\n\nThe full lifecycle — from doorstep to recycling mill\n\nStatus: Pilot app is built and live. All 10 steps below are implemented end-to-end — resident, operator, depot worker, depot manager, and admin roles all have working apps. A few integrations are running on stubs for the pilot (flagged below) and will be swapped for real vendors before scale.\n\n📸\n\nTry Before You Sign Up\n\nBefore creating an account, anyone can scan a few recyclables and see how much they'd earn. Scan a water bottle, a Coke can — the app shows points and dollar value instantly. Lowest time-to-value first screen.\n\n✅ BUILT · Scan UI + item flow live\n⚠️ Gemini 2.5 Flash not yet integrated — results mocked for pilot\n\n1\n\n2\n\n✍️\n\nSign Up & Order Bags\n\nCustomer creates an account and orders recycling bags. A welcome email goes out on signup. Bags come with a sheet of QR code stickers — each sticker has a unique QR code plus a printed number as backup. Customer sticks one label on each bag before filling it.\n\n💰 Revenue: bag sales (primary)\n✅ BUILT\n⚠️ Stripe stubbed — mock checkout for pilot\n\n♻️\n\nFill & Separate\n\nTwo bag types: Cardboard & Paper in one bag, Commingled (plastic, aluminum, tin, glass) in another. Separated earns 2x points. A quick recycling guide in the app shows good vs. bad examples — empty rinsed bottles vs. half-full ones, clean boxes vs. greasy pizza boxes.\n\n✅ BUILT · In-app recycling guide live\n\n3\n\n4\n\n📱\n\nScan QR & Schedule\n\nCustomer scans the QR sticker on the bag in the app, selects the bag type, takes a photo, and places it at the door. The app confirms their next scheduled pickup. If the QR is damaged, they type the printed number instead.\n\n✅ BUILT · jsQR scan + manual fallback + photo upload\n\n🔔\n\nText Notifications\n\nWhen a route is built, the resident gets an SMS + email with their delivery window. Then a text 30 minutes before pickup: \"Put your bags out!\" And a real-time alert when the driver is on the way: \"Your trashman is heading to you now.\" Extra time to grab one more bag if needed.\n\n✅ BUILT · Trigger logic + message log live\n⚠️ Twilio stubbed — messages log to DB for pilot\n\n5\n\n6\n\n🚐\n\nOperator Picks Up\n\nDriver follows the app route, stop by stop. At each address: one tap on the big \"Complete\" button to confirm pickup. Skip or flag issues with secondary buttons. Fast — no typing, no stopping. QR scan + manual number entry for detailed logging. Also delivers new bags and QR sticker sheets to residents who reordered.\n\n✅ BUILT · Operator app + route stepper live\n⚠️ Google Maps Routes API not yet integrated — stop order manual for pilot\n\n⚖️\n\nDepot: Open, Sort & Weigh\n\nAt the depot, worker scans the bag QR. Opens it on a table and separates materials. Each commodity is weighed individually — PET plastic, aluminum, tin, cardboard, paper — and entered into the app. Replaces the old paper-and-Excel process. No AI here yet; keep it simple and functional.\n\n✅ BUILT · 7-commodity weigh-in + contamination severity + live inventory\n\n7\n\n8\n\n⭐\n\nPoints Awarded\n\nPoints are auto-calculated based on each material's weight and current market price. The customer receives a text notification with their points earned. Contaminated or poorly recycled bags earn up to 90% less.\n\n✅ BUILT · Atomic ledger writes + balance updates on every bag\n\n🎁\n\nRedeem Gift Cards\n\n1,000 points = $10 gift card. Redeem for Amazon, Walmart, or local store gift cards. Can also apply credit toward their trash bill or donate to a nonprofit. Points are linked to real rewards from day one — no abstract numbers.\n\n✅ BUILT · Redemption requests + admin fulfillment queue\n⚠️ Cards sent manually by admin during pilot; Pay Trash Bill + Donate are disabled\n\n9\n\n10\n\n📦\n\nSell to Mills\n\nSorted materials are palletized and sold to domestic and international recycling mills at market rates (the \"yellow sheet\"). 7 commodity categories tracked: aluminum, tin, cardboard, paper, PET, HDPE, and mixed plastic.\n\n💰 Revenue: ~70% margin on sales\n✅ BUILT · Mill shipments + admin yellow-sheet price editor\n\n🏷️ QR Sticker Strategy\n\n1\n\nPilot: Plain bags + sticker sheets\nBuy generic bags. Print QR sticker sheets with a Rollo thermal printer. Each sticker has a unique QR code + printed number. Residents stick one label per bag. Cheapest way to start.\n\n2\n\nScale: Manufacturer-printed bags\nOnce revenue proves the model, work with a bag manufacturer to print QR codes directly on each bag. Eliminate the sticker step entirely.\n\n7 Accepted Materials\n\n🥫\n\nAluminum\n\n🥫\n\nTin / Steel\n\n📦\n\nCardboard\n\n📰\n\nPaper\n\n🧴\n\nPET Plastic\n\n🥛\n\nHDPE\n\n🧴\n\nMixed Plastic\n\nWho Uses the App\n\n🏠\n\nResident\n\nScans items to see value, orders bags, sticks QR labels on them, fills & separates, earns points, redeems gift cards, shares a personal refer-a-friend link\n\n🚐\n\nOperator\n\nFollows route, taps \"Complete\" at each stop, scans QR codes, delivers new bags + sticker sheets to residents\n\n⚖️\n\nDepot Worker\n\nScans bags, opens & sorts on table, weighs each material separately, enters data — replaces paper & Excel\n\n🏢\n\nDepot Manager\n\nMonitors depot inventory across locations, schedules mill pickups, reviews throughput reports\n\n📊\n\nAdmin\n\nCommand center — all zones, all users, revenue, contamination alerts, commodity pricing, compliance notices, plus an admin-only in-app AI Assistant that answers questions about the app, grounded in these user guides\n\nHow the Money Flows\n\n1\n\nCustomer buys recycling bags + QR sticker sheets\n\n$ In (Primary)\n\n2\n\nSorted materials sold to recycling mills at market rate\n\n$ In\n\n3\n\nCustomers redeem ~30% of material value as gift cards\n\n$ Out\n\n4\n\n~70% margin kept on every material transaction\n\n$ Profit\n\n5\n\nLong-term: franchise fees from new depot territories\n\n$ Scale\n\nPoints & Rewards System\n\n1,000 pts\n=\n$10 gift card\n\nSignup bonus: 10 pts · ~5 pts per bag · Separated = 2x points · Commingled = base points · Contaminated = up to 90% reduction\n\nPoints linked to real rewards from day one — no abstract numbers. Bonus points and multiplier weeks to build the habit early.\n\n🛒 Amazon Gift Card\n🏪 Walmart Gift Card\n🧾 Pay Trash Bill\n❤️ Donate to Nonprofit\n\n♻️ Recycling Education\n\nSimple good-vs-bad examples in the app help residents recycle correctly. Correct recycling = more points. Contamination = lost earnings.\n\nGOOD\n\nEmpty rinsed bottle\n\nFlattened dry box\n\nBAD\n\nHalf-full soda bottle\n\nGreasy pizza box\n\nMore tutorials and prep tips coming as we collect real user data. AI-powered scanning to be added after the pilot to help identify items.\n\nWhat's Next\n\nThe pilot app is complete and ready to run. Everything below is intentionally deferred — we want real pilot data before spending money or engineering effort on these pieces.\n\n💳\n\nReal Stripe checkout\n\nSwap the mock bag-order checkout for live Stripe payments. Interface is already abstracted — one adapter file to change.\n\nWaiting on pilot pricing validation before going live.\n\n📱\n\nReal Twilio SMS\n\nDrop in Twilio credentials so \"put your bags out\" and \"driver on the way\" actually text residents. All trigger points are already wired.\n\nCurrently logs every outgoing message to Firestore for QA.\n\n🎁\n\nAutomated gift card fulfillment\n\nReplace admin-manual gift card sending with Amazon Incentives API + Walmart API. Admin queue is already built.\n\nManual fulfillment is fine at 100 pilot residents; automate when redemption volume justifies it.\n\n🧾\n\nPay Trash Bill + Donate\n\nEnable the two disabled redemption options. Requires billing-provider integration and nonprofit partner selection.\n\nAmazon/Walmart covers 90% of pilot demand.\n\n🤖\n\nAI contamination detection at depot\n\nPhoto-based auto-suggest of contamination severity when the depot worker opens a bag — speeds up processing and reduces subjective calls.\n\nNeed pilot photos to train/validate first.\n\n🏷️\n\nManufacturer-printed QR bags\n\nMove from Rollo sticker sheets to bags with QR codes printed directly by the manufacturer. Eliminates the sticker-application step for residents.\n\nSticker model keeps pilot cheap; switch once unit economics justify MOQ.\n\n📲\n\nNative mobile app\n\nThe current app is a mobile-optimized web app that works great on phones. A native iOS/Android version (separate repo) can come later for push notifications and camera polish.\n\nWeb app covers the full resident + operator flow today.\n\n🗺️\n\nMulti-zone rollout UI\n\nData model already supports multiple zones and depots. Admin UI currently focuses on the single pilot zone — expand to multi-zone dashboards when we grow beyond one HOA.\n\nSingle-zone UI = simpler pilot experience.\n\n📡\n\nGPS driver-on-the-way\n\nToday the operator taps a button when heading to a stop. A GPS geofence could auto-trigger the resident text when the truck gets close.\n\nTap is reliable and zero-battery; upgrade after we validate operator behavior.\n\n⚖️\n\nBluetooth/USB scale integration\n\nDepot workers currently type weights manually. Auto-capture from a connected scale speeds up processing and removes typos.\n\nManual entry is fast enough at pilot volume.\n\n🏪\n\nFranchise / multi-depot operations\n\nTools for onboarding new depot territories — franchise agreements, per-depot KPIs, cross-depot logistics. All revenue model #5 on the diagram above.\n\nPost-pilot milestone.\n\n📬\n\nAutomated compliance mailing\n\nAdmin generates compliance notice PDFs today and clicks \"mark as mailed.\" Next step: integrate a direct-mail API (Lob or similar) to actually send them.\n\nVolume is too low pre-pilot to justify integration.\n\nAccounts to Set Up\n\nThird-party services needed to swap out the pilot stubs and keep the app running. Pricing estimates assume ~100 pilot residents; costs scale roughly linearly from there.\n\nREPLACES STRIPE STUB\n💳\n\nStripe\n\nLive payments for bag orders and QR sticker-sheet reorders. Create a standard account, add business details, drop in API keys.\n\n2.9% + $0.30 per transaction\nNo monthly fee · ~$1 fee on a $25 bag order\n\nREPLACES TWILIO STUB\n📱\n\nTwilio\n\nSMS for \"put your bags out,\" \"driver on the way,\" and points-earned notifications. Needs one provisioned phone number + A2P 10DLC registration for US.\n\n~$10–15/mo at pilot scale\n$1.15/mo per number + $0.0083/SMS + one-time ~$15 A2P registration\n\nNEW — NEEDED FOR INVITES\n📧\n\nMailerSend (or Resend)\n\nTransactional email: operator/depot/admin invite links, redemption receipts, password resets. Needs a verified sending domain (DKIM/SPF).\n\nFree up to 3,000 emails/mo\nPaid tier ~$28/mo for 50k — pilot fits in free tier\n\nALREADY SET UP\n🔥\n\nFirebase (Blaze plan)\n\nAuth, Firestore, Storage. Blaze is required because the app calls external APIs (Gemini, Maps) from server routes. Pay-as-you-go beyond generous free quotas.\n\n~$0–5/mo at pilot scale\nEffectively free until ~thousands of users\n\nALREADY SET UP\n🤖\n\nGoogle AI — Gemini 2.5 Flash\n\nVision model for the pre-signup scan feature. Billed per token; images are counted as input tokens.\n\n~$20–50/mo at pilot scale\n$0.075 / 1M input tokens · $0.30 / 1M output · ~1¢ per scan\n\nALREADY SET UP\n🗺️\n\nGoogle Maps — Routes API\n\nOptimized pickup routes for the operator app ( computeRoutes with waypoint optimization). Billed per request.\n\n~$1–10/mo at pilot scale\n$10 per 1,000 optimized route requests · one route/day = ~$0.30/mo\n\nHOSTING\n▲\n\nVercel\n\nHosts the Next.js app + API routes. Free Hobby tier works for the pilot, but commercial use technically requires Pro.\n\n$0 (Hobby) or $20/mo (Pro)\nUpgrade to Pro before taking real payments\n\nREPLACES GIFT CARD STUB\n🛒\n\nAmazon Incentives API\n\nAutomated Amazon gift card fulfillment instead of admin-manual sending. Requires business application + prepaid balance.\n\n0% fee — cards at face value\n~$500+ minimum deposit · approval takes ~2–4 weeks\n\nREPLACES GIFT CARD STUB\n🏪\n\nWalmart Gift Card Program\n\nAutomated Walmart gift card fulfillment. Typically accessed through a reseller (Tango, Blackhawk) since Walmart has no public direct API.\n\n~0–4% markup via reseller\nTango / Blackhawk bundles Amazon, Walmart, Target, etc. in one API\n\nFUTURE — COMPLIANCE\n✉️\n\nLob (direct mail)\n\nAutomates mailing compliance notices that are currently generated as PDFs and marked \"mailed\" by admin. Skip until volume justifies.\n\n~$1–2 per letter\nCertified mail +$4–5 · no monthly minimums\n\nONE-TIME HARDWARE\n🖨️\n\nRollo thermal printer + labels\n\nPrints QR sticker sheets for residents. One per depot. Uses cheap thermal label rolls — no ink, no toner.\n\n~$180 printer + ~$30/roll\n5,000 labels per roll = ~$0.006 per sticker\n\nPilot Plan\n\n~100\n\nResidents\n\n1\n\nLocal HOA\n\n$1K\n\nGift Card Budget\n\n~2 wks\n\nTo Customers\n\nTarget a single HOA near Kirk. Deliver bags and QR stickers in person. Collect feedback face-to-face. Iterate based on real usage data. Train AI models later once volume justifies cost.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash — Pilot v1.2 · Build complete · Updated April 23, 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Phase 10 QR Generation Guide (phase-10-qr-generation.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 10 · QR sticker generation\n\nWhat Tia sees after Phase 9 lands: a new 🏷️ Bag stickers tab in the admin\nsidebar that pre-prints the QR sticker sheets the whole system relies on. Each sheet is 10\nlabels sized for a Rollo thermal printer, with a scannable QR plus a printed-number\nfallback. This is the fix for a bootstrap problem the earlier phases papered over: until\nnow, sticker sheets only existed as physical paper; the app did not know about them until\nan operator scanned one at delivery. Phase 10 moves the QR codes into Firestore first,\nthen prints them.\n\n⚠️ Hardware requirement\nTesting this end-to-end needs a real thermal printer. If you don't have one:\n\n• Without a Rollo printer — you can still generate sheets, inspect the PDF preview, and verify the Firestore records get created. The \"Printed\" state can also be triggered by clicking Mark as printed in the admin UI.\n\n• No real money or external services are involved in this flow.\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• Tia can generate a new sticker sheet with one click — the app picks a unique 4-digit sheet number and writes the stickerSheets + 10 bags records atomically.\n\n• The print view renders 10 labels, each with a QR code and the printed number ( BAG-4237-01 through BAG-4237-10 ), sized for Rollo 2.25″×1.25″ thermal label stock.\n\n• Printing marks the sheet's printedAt timestamp so the admin knows which sheets are ready to hand to operators.\n\n• When Aguirre the operator scans the first sticker at delivery, the same 10 bag docs get claimed for the resident — no new bags created, no duplicates.\n\n• The admin list view correctly reflects each sheet's lifecycle: Awaiting print → Printed · unassigned → Issued .\n\n📋 Before you start\nDevice: 💻 Laptop (Tia) — Scenarios 1–4 only need this.\n\nDependencies — only Scenario 5 has prereqs; Scenarios 1–4 are standalone:\n\n• Scenarios 1–4 : just Tia signed in. No other setup needed.\n\n• Scenario 5 only : a pending bag order on an in-progress route (same prereqs as Phase 6 ). Skip this scenario if you just want to sanity-check the print view.\n\nScenario 1 — Generate your first sticker sheet\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia on a laptop.\n\n• Open /admin/bags (or click 🏷️ Bag stickers in the sidebar).\n\n• Observe the three status tiles — Awaiting print , Ready to deliver , Issued . On a fresh install all three are 0.\n\n• Click Generate new sheet . The button spins briefly, then the browser navigates to the print view for the new sheet.\n\nExpected: A brand-new print view opens at\n/admin/bags/<sheetId>/print showing a #XXXX sheet number\n(4 digits) and 10 labels labelled BAG-XXXX-01 through BAG-XXXX-10 .\nUnder the hood the API reserved that sheet number, created the stickerSheets doc\nwith residentId: null , and created 10 bags docs each with\nstatus: \"unused\" and residentId: null .\n\nVerify:\n\nQR codes look like machine-readable squares, not a broken image\n\nThe printed number under each QR matches the same format BAG-XXXX-NN\n\nThe sheet number across all 10 labels is the same (e.g. all BAG-4237-* ), index runs 01 through 10\n\nScenario 2 — Print the sheet\n\nAdmin On the same print view.\n\n• Click Print + mark at the top of the page. The browser print dialog opens.\n\n• In the dialog, verify the paper size shows as 2.25 × 1.25 in (or your browser's equivalent label preset).\n\n• Choose Save as PDF for a first test so you can inspect the output without burning label stock.\n\n• Open the saved PDF. Each of the 10 labels should occupy its own 2.25″×1.25″ page.\n\nExpected: The browser sends 10 pages to the printer. The admin sidebar,\nthe print toolbar, and the \"Preview below shows all 10 labels…\" note all disappear in the\nprint output. After the print dialog closes, the page refreshes and the subtitle now\nreads \"last printed <timestamp>\" . The Firestore stickerSheets doc\nnow has a printedAt value.\n\nVerify:\n\nEach label prints on its own page — no two labels share a sheet\n\nThe QR on each label looks dark and crisp in the saved PDF (not blurry or rasterized)\n\nThe subtitle flips from \"not yet printed\" to the printed timestamp after the dialog closes\n\nReopening the page the next day still shows the printed timestamp (persisted)\n\nScenario 3 — Read the admin list view\n\nAdmin Back to /admin/bags .\n\n• Reload the page.\n\n• The sheet you just printed should be the top row.\n\nExpected: The row shows the sheet number (monospace), a sky-blue\nPrinted · unassigned badge, an empty resident column, and timestamps in the\nCreated and Printed columns. The top status tiles now show 1 in Ready to deliver\nand 0 in Awaiting print .\n\nVerify:\n\nGenerating a second sheet before printing it increments the Awaiting print tile to 1 and drops its badge to the amber Awaiting print pill\n\nThe Print view button on each row opens that sheet's print page directly\n\nSheets sort newest-first\n\nScenario 4 — Unique-number guard\n\nAdmin Rapid-fire test.\n\n• Click Generate new sheet several times in a row (5–10 sheets).\n\n• After each generation finishes, go back to /admin/bags and skim the Sheet # column.\n\nExpected: Every sheet has a distinct 4-digit number. The API retries up\nto 8 times if it randomly lands on a colliding number. At pilot scale (a few hundred\nsheets) collisions will be rare; if you ever see a 503 response with\nsheet_number_exhausted , we've run out of the 9000-number space and the reservation\nscheme needs to grow to 5+ digits.\n\nVerify:\n\nNo two rows share the same Sheet #\n\nEach sheet's 10 bag codes are unique across the whole bags collection\n\nScenario 5 — Operator claims the sheet at delivery\n\nAdmin + Operator Two devices (or two tabs).\n\nThis is the end-to-end handoff. It requires a pending bag order already enqueued on\ntoday's in-progress route — follow Phase 5 and Phase 6 to set that up if you don't have\none.\n\n• As Tia, grab one of the Printed · unassigned sheets from the list. Open its print view and note the first sticker's code, e.g. BAG-4237-01 .\n\n• As Aguirre the operator, start the route and navigate to the bag-order delivery stop for a resident.\n\n• At the deliver step, scan or type BAG-4237-01 and submit.\n\n• Switch back to Tia and reload /admin/bags .\n\nExpected: The sheet's row flips to a green Issued badge, the\nResident column now shows the receiving resident's name, and the Issued column fills in\nwith today's timestamp. In Firestore, the same 10 bags docs that were created in\nScenario 1 are still there — their residentId field changed from null to the\nresident's uid (no new docs, no duplicates). The stickerSheets doc also has\nresidentId and bagOrderId set.\n\nVerify:\n\nBag count in Firestore's bags collection did not grow by 10 — the existing docs were updated, not recreated\n\nTrying to re-deliver the same sheet to a different bag order returns sheet_already_issued\n\nScanning a sheet number that was never generated (e.g. BAG-9999-01 ) returns sheet_not_pre_issued\n\nScenario 6 — Resident scans a claimed bag\n\nResident Same resident the sheet was just delivered to.\n\n• Open the resident app on the phone and go to /resident/scan-bag .\n\n• Scan or enter one of the codes from the delivered sheet (e.g. BAG-4237-03 ).\n\n• Complete the flow — declaration + doorstep photo → submit.\n\nExpected: The pickup creates successfully. This confirms the delivery\nhandoff is complete: the bag exists, belongs to this resident, and is in unused\nstatus ready to be claimed. Before Phase 10 this worked too, because the deliver\nendpoint created the bag docs on-scan; after Phase 10 it still works, because the same\ndocs are claimed instead.\n\nVerify:\n\nScanning a sticker from a sheet that was never delivered returns bag_not_yours (the bag has residentId=null; the resident is not null; inequality rejects)\n\nScanning a sticker from another resident's delivered sheet still returns bag_not_yours\n\nQuick troubleshooting\n\nWhat you see\nLikely cause\nWhat to try\n\n\"Generate new sheet\" errors out with sheet_number_exhausted\nThe 1000–9999 space has enough collisions that 8 retries in a row all hit existing sheets. Only realistic after thousands of sheets.\nOutgrow the 4-digit scheme — widen SHEET_NUMBER_MAX in /api/qr-sheet/route.ts to 99999.\n\nQR codes render as plain text or a broken <svg>\nqrcode server render failed silently or the SVG markup is being stripped.\nCheck the print-view server logs for an exception from QRCode.toString . Confirm the dependency is installed and the route runs in nodejs runtime, not edge.\n\nPrint output cuts off or is wildly the wrong size\nThe browser ignored the @page 2.25 × 1.25in size and defaulted to Letter.\nIn the print dialog, under More settings , set paper size to a custom 2.25 × 1.25 inch or pick the Rollo preset. Some browsers need \"Save as PDF\" to honour custom @page sizes.\n\nOperator scan returns sheet_not_pre_issued on a sticker you just printed\nThe printed label is reading as a different sheet number (e.g. smudged scan) or the sheet was deleted from Firestore.\nType the printed number manually instead of scanning — if the typed code works, the camera misread the QR. If typed also fails, verify the stickerSheets doc still exists.\n\nRow shows Printed · unassigned but Tia never clicked Print\nThe Print + mark button also fires when Tia uses the browser's own print shortcut on the preview page, as long as the endpoint call succeeds afterward.\nNo action needed — this is intentional so the printed timestamp reflects the latest actual print attempt.\n\nAdmin list view is empty even though API calls succeeded\nThe page is server-rendered — its cached version may be stale.\nReload the tab. It's already force-dynamic , so a reload is enough; no deploy needed.\n\nWhat's not testable yet\n\nPhase 10 only covers generating stickers. Bulk-printing N sheets at once,\nre-printing a subset of labels from an existing sheet, voiding a sheet that got damaged, and\nintegrating a real Rollo driver (vs the browser print dialog) are all future work. The\nsheet-number space is also deliberately small (1000–9999) for the pilot — the next revision\nwill likely switch to a monotonically increasing counter once we have more than a few\nhundred sheets in play. For now, Tia just clicks Generate whenever operators need more\nstickers, prints, and hands the physical sheets to the route.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 10 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Phase 11 Post-Deploy Smoke Test (phase-11-security-deploy.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 11 · Post-deploy smoke test\n\nAfter the dev team pushes the production build, do this 5-minute check to confirm nothing\nobvious broke. No new features — you're just proving every role can still sign in and open\nthe pages they use every day.\n\n⚠️ Heads up\nThis smoke test is sign-in + page-load only . You're not exercising the stubs (Stripe, SMS, gift cards, compliance mailings). If a mock screen appears (e.g. the Stripe \"paid\" mock), that's expected — treat it as \"page loaded\" and move on.\n\nBefore you start.\nThe dev team will tell you \"the production deploy is live — please smoke-test.\"\nThey'll give you the production URL. Use that URL for every step below. If you can't sign\nin at all on the production URL, stop and tell the dev team — don't continue.\n\nThe smoke test (≈5 minutes)\n\nSign in as each role, open the pages listed, and confirm they load without a red error\nbanner. Tick the box when a role passes. If anything fails, note which role and which page\nand send it back to the dev team.\n\nAdmin Tia — 💻 laptop\n\nSign in as Tia. Open each of these pages in turn. Each one should render without an error banner:\n\n• /admin — dashboard with numbers (not placeholders)\n\n• /admin/users — list of users\n\n• /admin/zones — Kirk pilot zone shows up\n\n• /admin/routes — route list loads\n\n• /admin/bags — sticker sheet list loads\n\n• /admin/pricing — 7 materials with prices\n\n• /admin/redemptions — gift-card queue loads\n\n• /admin/compliance — compliance batches loads\n\nVerify:\n\nAll admin pages load. No red error banners.\n\nResident Resident Aguirre — 📱 phone\n\nSign in as resident.aguirre@example.com . Open each page:\n\n• /resident — dashboard with points balance\n\n• /resident/order-bags — order form loads\n\n• /resident/rewards — transaction history loads\n\n• /resident/calculator — payout calculator loads\n\n• /resident/profile — profile page loads\n\nVerify:\n\nAll resident pages load. Points balance shows up.\n\nOperator Operator Aguirre — 📱 phone\n\nSign in as operator.aguirre@wbct-pilot.test . Open:\n\n• /operator — today's routes (may be empty, that's fine)\n\nVerify:\n\nOperator page loads without an error.\n\nDepot Depot Goode — 💻 laptop\n\nSign in as depot.goode@wbct-pilot.test . Open each page:\n\n• /depot — depot home\n\n• /depot/incoming — incoming deliveries list\n\n• /depot/inventory — 7 materials shown\n\nVerify:\n\nAll depot pages load. Inventory lists the 7 materials.\n\nIf something doesn't work\n\nWhat you see\nWhat to do\n\nA page loads but shows a red banner mentioning permission or access .\nScreenshot the page + URL and send to the dev team. Don't try to fix it.\n\nA page shows a spinner forever, or a blank screen.\nWait 30 seconds, then reload once. If it still doesn't load, screenshot + report.\n\nYou can't sign in at all on the production URL.\nStop the smoke test. Tell the dev team — they probably missed an env var on Vercel.\n\nA page that worked 5 minutes ago now throws an error.\nCould be a still-building search index. Wait 2–3 minutes and retry. If it keeps failing, report.\n\nWhat \"passes\" means.\nYou're not testing features here — just that the lights are on. If all four role cards above\nhave a ticked checkbox and you didn't see any red banners, reply to the dev team with\n\"smoke test passed\" and the time you ran it. That's the whole handoff.\n\nDevelopers: the deep-dive version of this guide (Firestore rules, composite indexes,\nrules playground, Vercel env config) lives in\nphase-11-security-deploy.DEV.html .\n\n← Previous · Step 8\nDepot flow\n\nNext · Step 10\nPilot launch checklist →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 11 smoke test · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Phase 12 Pilot Launch Checklist (phase-12-pilot-launch.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 12 · Pilot launch\n\nThe code is shipped — this phase is physical. Print stickers, hand-deliver the first\nbags, sign up ~5 neighbors in person, and watch them try the app. Everything below is\na real-world checklist, not a test plan. Cross items off as you go; re-open the guide\nthe next time you grow the pilot to another block.\n\n⚠️ Pilot stubs that affect real-world launch ops\nYou're going live with real neighbors, but these still aren't real:\n\n• Bag orders are free in-app — Stripe is mocked. Tell pilot residents their first box of bags is on the house during the trial.\n\n• Gift cards fulfill manually — when a resident redeems, you'll see it in your admin queue. Email the card yourself.\n\n• SMS is console-logged, not sent. During the pilot, tell residents what to expect verbally (\"we'll knock when we're on the way\") since they won't get the text.\n\n• Compliance notices — print the generated PDF, mail it, then click \"Mark as mailed\" in admin.\n\nBefore day one\n\nBuy the hardware\n\nRollo thermal printer — X1038 model or equivalent USB direct-thermal.\n$180-ish. You do not need the Rollo Ship cloud software; the app talks to it via the\nbrowser print dialog with a custom 2.25 × 1.25 page size.\n\nRollo-compatible labels — 2.25″×1.25″ direct thermal, 1000/roll. Buy\ntwo rolls; one resident chews through ~5 sheets per year.\n\nPostal scale — a $20 digital kitchen scale is fine for pilot. Depot\nprocessing is manual weight entry; precision to the ounce is enough.\n\nBags — the physical recycling bag. Clear contractor bags (42-gal) are\nthe pilot default; order 100 to start. Stickers apply to the bag, not to a box.\n\nRollo + USB cable\n\n2 rolls of 2.25×1.25 labels\n\nDigital scale (grams or ounces both work)\n\n100 clear 42-gal bags\n\nPermanent marker (for writing the printed-number fallback if a QR smudges)\n\nLock the pilot footprint\n\nThe seed script ( npm run seed:pilot ) creates one zone\n( Kirk pilot , ZIP 80301, Wednesday pickup) and one depot\n( Kirk Depot ). Before onboarding residents:\n\n• Open /admin/zones and confirm the ZIP list actually matches the\nblock you're piloting. If you're piloting a different ZIP, edit the zone in the\nadmin UI (or edit scripts/seed-pilot.ts and re-seed).\n\n• Confirm the depot address on the zone matches where you physically process bags\n(your garage, a friend's warehouse, etc.). This address appears on the compliance\nletters.\n\n• Set pricing once at /admin/pricing . The seed defaults\n(aluminum $0.92, cardboard $0.08, etc.) are reasonable spring-2026 numbers but\ncommodity prices move — check ScrapMonster the morning you launch.\n\nPrint enough stickers for week one\n\nAt /admin/bags , click Generate new sheet five times. Each\nsheet = 10 stickers = one resident's starter set. Five residents → five sheets. Print\neach sheet ( Print + mark ) and keep them labeled by sheet number in a folder.\n\nDon't print all 50 at once on the first run — a jammed roll or misaligned template\ncan eat a bunch of label stock. Print one, inspect the output (QR scans cleanly? number\nlegible?), then print the rest.\n\nOne test sheet printed and physically verified (scan a sticker with your phone)\n\n4 more sheets printed after the test passes\n\nSheets sorted by sheet number and stored separately from the bag pile\n\nDay one — onboarding the first 5 residents\n\nPre-walk the block\n\nBefore knocking, have Tia generate Initial Service Notice compliance letters\nfor the zone at /admin/compliance and drop them in the recipients'\nmailboxes 2–3 days ahead. The letter explains the service and sets expectations so the\ndoorstep conversation is shorter.\n\nLegally the letter is what gets you past some municipalities' residential\nservice rules — don't skip it even if you know the resident.\n\nThe doorstep pitch (~90 seconds)\n\nSay this, roughly:\n\n\"Hi — I'm Nicolas, I sent the notice on Tuesday. We're running a weekly recycling\npickup for this block. I take your separated recyclables on Wednesday, weigh them at\nmy depot, and you get points worth ~30% of market value. No cost, no contract. I need\n90 seconds to set you up and hand you your first bags — can I?\"\n\nThen, on your phone (or theirs):\n\n• Open /signup on the resident's phone. Help them create an email\n+ password (Google works too). Enter their address and phone.\n\n• Wait for the welcome screen — they should see 10 welcome points\n(worth $0.10).\n\n• Hand them one pre-printed sticker sheet plus 10 clear\nbags . Write their last name on the sheet with the marker (you reference this\non delivery).\n\n• Walk them through the Phase 3 loop on their phone: tap a sticker → open\nScan Bag → declare sorted/mixed → take doorstep photo → submit . Do the first\nbag with them so they don't stall on Wednesday.\n\n• Point at the Rewards tab so they know where points accumulate.\n\nSet expectations out loud: \"Bags out by 7am Wednesday. I'll text you when I'm\non my way. Points land within an hour of pickup.\"\n\nMark the stickers claimed\n\nYou physically handed the sheet to the resident, but the app doesn't know yet —\nstickers are in the Printed · unassigned state until the operator (you) scans\none at delivery. Two options:\n\n• Clean option : create a route for tomorrow with a single \"deliver\nbag order\" stop per resident, start the route in /operator , and scan the\nfirst sticker on each sheet to claim it. This is the production flow.\n\n• Pilot shortcut : use npm run dev:issue-bags -- <resident-email>\nfrom your laptop. This issues a sheet in a single command — faster for the first 5\nresidents but bypasses the operator flow, so you won't test it until week two.\n\nPick one and use it consistently — mixing the two makes the sheet numbers hard to\nreconcile.\n\nWeek one — first pickup cycle\n\nTuesday evening\n\nBuild tomorrow's route at /admin/routes :\n\n• Click New route , pick the zone + tomorrow's date.\n\n• Click Optimize order . The route runs through Google Maps and returns a\nwaypoint order starting from the depot.\n\n• Assign the operator (yourself). Click Save .\n\n• Text the residents manually the first week: \"Pickup tomorrow — bag out\nbefore 7am, sticker facing the street.\"\n\nWednesday morning\n\nOn your phone:\n\n• Open /operator , tap today's route, hit Start route .\n\n• Drive to the first stop. Tap Driver on the way if you want the SMS stub\nto fire (shows up in smsLog ; real Twilio is still post-pilot).\n\n• At the curb: scan the QR on the bag, confirm the declaration/photo match, tap\nComplete . If the bag is not out, tap Not out .\n\n• After the last stop, tap Deliver to Depot . The route is now ready for\nprocessing.\n\nWednesday afternoon — processing\n\nAt the depot (garage), open /depot on a laptop:\n\n• Incoming shows today's returned route. Tap into it.\n\n• For each bag: scan the QR, weigh each of the 7 material piles on the scale,\nenter weights, pick contamination severity (be honest — even low levels), submit.\n\n• Watch the resident get their SMS stub + points ledger entry. On their end, the\nRewards page updates.\n\nAim for under 5 minutes per bag the first time. You'll get faster. Keep notes on\npaper for anything that felt awkward — that's Phase 13.\n\nWhat to watch for\n\nSignal\nWhat it means\nFix\n\nResident can't find the Scan button\nNav hierarchy or icon isn't obvious enough.\nPatch src/app/resident/page.tsx — probably surface Scan as a primary CTA.\n\nQR scans fail in low light (porch at 7am)\nCamera auto-exposure / QR contrast.\nIncrease QR error-correction level in /api/qr-sheet (currently M — try Q or H). Reprint affected sheets.\n\nResident forgets the bag\nReminder cadence is wrong.\nNote frequency; next iteration wires up a Tuesday-evening SMS reminder (Twilio un-stub).\n\nWeigh-in takes >5 min/bag\nThe 7-material form is slow when the bag only has 2 material types.\nFuture patch: collapse empty rows, add a \"skip rest of form\" shortcut.\n\nPoints feel abstract — resident asks \"what's this worth?\"\nRewards page doesn't translate points-to-dollars prominently enough.\nCheck src/app/resident/rewards/page.tsx ; expand the conversion label.\n\nStripe/Twilio/gift cards stubbed out\nKnown — pilot ships without payments + SMS + real rewards.\nUn-stub in this order post-pilot: Twilio SMS → Stripe bag orders → gift card fulfillment. Each lives behind its adapter file in src/lib/* .\n\nEnd of week one — retro\n\nSit down Wednesday night and write:\n\n• Friction moments — each place a resident (or you) hesitated, fumbled, or had to be talked through something. Raw — one line each.\n\n• Data sanity — spot-check Firestore: do all 5 residents have a signup_bonus transaction plus a pickup transaction for this week? Does pointsBalance match the sum of their transactions? Any orphan docs?\n\n• Unit economics — total weight collected × customer price × customerPct. Subtract Rollo labels, bag cost, gas. Is the unit economics roughly sane at 5 residents? Extrapolate to 50.\n\n• Decision — grow the zone by another 5 next week, or tighten the existing flow first.\n\nGrowing past the first block\n\nWhen the pilot zone is working, add residents, not zones. 20–30 residents in one Kirk zone\nstresses the app more than 5 residents across 3 zones. The real multi-zone work is\nseparate — it touches route optimization, operator assignment, and the admin-per-zone\ncompliance cadence, and it's deliberately out of scope until the single zone is stable.\n\nWhen you do grow, re-run npm run seed:pilot is a no-op — use the admin UI to\nadd zones/depots, not the seed script. The seed is only for bootstrapping empty state.\n\n← Previous · Step 9\nPost-deploy smoke test\n\nDone · back to\nTesting roadmap →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 12 pilot launch checklist · Kirk pilot · April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Resident App Guide (phase-3-resident.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 3 · Resident App\n\nEverything a homeowner can do: estimate their trash value before signing up, order recycling\nbags, scan a bag before pickup, check their points, and redeem a gift card. All scenarios\nin this guide are run as Resident Aguirre unless noted.\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nSome features look real but don't fully fire during the pilot. That's by design — please\ndon't file these as bugs:\n\n• Ordering bags — checkout runs through Stripe's hosted test checkout. Use Stripe test cards only; no live card is charged during the pilot.\n\n• Redeeming a gift card — the request lands in an admin queue; the actual gift card is emailed manually by an admin, not auto-fulfilled. Pilot-gated: each resident can redeem once, and Redeem buttons lock once the pilot cap is reached.\n\n• \"Pay trash bill\" and \"Donate\" buttons are disabled on purpose.\n\n• SMS notifications are logged to the server console, not sent by text during the pilot.\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• A visitor can try the AI scan before signing up.\n\n• A signed-in resident's dashboard shows the right numbers (points, next pickup).\n\n• Ordering bags produces an order confirmation.\n\n• Scanning a bag before pickup day registers it as \"out for pickup.\"\n\n• The payout calculator reflects current material pricing.\n\n• Gift card redemptions show up in the admin queue (cross-checked in the Admin guide).\n\nScenario 1 — Pre-signup AI scan (visitor, no account yet)\n\nVisitor No one signed in.\n\n• Open the homepage in a private window on your phone.\n\n• Tap Try the scan (or the big camera button on the landing page).\n\n•\nAllow camera access when the browser asks.\n\n•\nPoint the camera at a small pile of recyclables (a soda can, a cardboard box, a\nplastic bottle) and tap the shutter.\n\n• Wait a few seconds.\n\nExpected: A list of detected items appears, each with an estimated\nweight and an estimated dollar value. A total at the bottom shows what the pile would\nearn. A Sign up to claim button is below the results.\n\nVerify:\n\nCamera opens and captures an image\n\nAI returns a list of items (not an error)\n\nClosing the tab and reopening shows no saved scan (it's meant to be session-only)\n\nThe estimate won't be perfect.\nThe AI is rough on purpose — its job is to show the resident that their trash has real\nvalue, not to be accurate to the cent. If it labels a can as a bottle, note it, but\ndon't call it a bug.\n\nScenario 1b — Signing up (NEW · Jun 9)\n\nVisitor No account yet.\n\n• From the landing page, tap Sign up to claim (or Sign up ).\n\n• On the Four simple things step, read the neo-brutalist sticker cards (white cards with ink borders, numbered badges, and mint/peach chips highlighting the free-delivery perk).\n\n• On the address step, start typing your street address. Google Places Autocomplete suggests addresses and auto-fills street, city, state, and ZIP when you pick one. Unit/apt is still typed in by hand.\n\n• Confirm that once a ZIP is filled in, the page shows the matched pickup day(s) for that zone (a zone can have more than one pickup day per week).\n\n• Finish signup and land on the resident home.\n\nExpected: Address autocomplete fills the fields cleanly, the matched\npickup day(s) appear after the ZIP is entered, and the new account receives a\nwelcome email shortly after creation.\n\nVerify:\n\nAddress autocomplete suggests and auto-fills street/city/state/zip (unit/apt stays manual)\n\nMatched pickup day(s) show after the ZIP is entered — more than one day if the zone has multiple\n\n\"Four simple things\" renders as sticker cards with numbered badges and the free-delivery chip\n\nA welcome email arrives at the signup address\n\nScenario 2 — The dashboard\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Sign in as Aguirre and land on the resident home.\n\n• Read each card top to bottom.\n\nYou should see:\n\n• A points balance tile (10 right after signup) and a value tile.\n\n• NEW (Apr 27): A redemption-progress card just below the tiles — \"X pts to a $10 gift card\" with a thin progress bar. Tap it to jump to /resident/rewards.\n\n• A next pickup card with either a date or \"Pickup day not assigned yet.\"\n\n• NEW (Jun 9): A Next delivery card showing the delivery time window once a resident is on an admin-built delivery route (admin picks the window when building the route; residents also get SMS + email with it).\n\n• NEW (Apr 27): If a pricing campaign is live (admin set one in Phase 4 · Scenario 5c), a 🔥 BONUS RUNNING NOW card lists the active multipliers.\n\n• An Order more bags CTA — or Order your first box until at least one bag order has been placed (Apr 27 copy change).\n\n• A notification or impact card further down.\n\n• A bottom nav bar: Home · Scan · Calculator · Guide · Rewards .\n\nExpected: Everything loads in one pass, no long spinners. Numbers match\nwhat you'd expect for a brand-new account (10 pts, no pickups yet).\n\n🎉 Signup-bonus modal (NEW · Apr 27)\n\nOn the very first dashboard visit after signup, a modal pops over the screen explaining\n\"You earned 10 points — that's your signup bonus, worth $0.10 toward your first\nreward.\" Tap Let's go or click outside to dismiss. The modal is\nkeyed by uid in localStorage ( wbct.signupBonusAcked.<uid> ) —\nit never reappears for the same browser. Clear that key in DevTools to re-test.\n\nVerify:\n\nPoints balance correct\n\nBottom nav switches screens cleanly\n\nNEW: Signup-bonus modal shows once and never again on the same browser/account\n\nNEW: Redemption progress bar reads correctly — at 10 pts it shows \"990 pts to a $10 gift card\" and a 1% bar\n\nNEW: Order CTA reads \"Order your first box\" until you've placed an order, then flips to \"Order more bags\"\n\nNEW: If admin started a campaign (Phase 4 · Scenario 5c), the 🔥 banner is visible above the order CTA\n\nNEW: Once on a delivery route, the \"Next delivery\" card shows the time window, and SMS + email with that window are sent (SMS is logged to the console during the pilot)\n\nScenario 3 — Ordering bags\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Tap Order more bags on the home screen.\n\n• Use the + / – stepper to choose a quantity. Try 1 first.\n\n• Read the order summary. With 1 box, you should see a $10 delivery line because the order is under $20.\n\n• Bump the quantity to 3. Delivery should drop to Free once the subtotal clears $20.\n\n• Tap Pay now — checkout runs through Stripe's hosted page.\n\nStripe checkout (NEW · Jun 9)\n\nPay now hands off to Stripe's hosted checkout. Orders whose welcome\ncredit fully covers the cart ($0 due) skip Stripe entirely and go straight to the\nsuccess page.\n\nExpected: After paying on Stripe (or instantly, for a $0 welcome-credit\norder), you land on an order confirmation / success screen with an order number and a\n\"delivered on next pickup\" line. The\norder appears in Aguirre's account history.\n\nVerify:\n\nDelivery rule toggles at the $20 threshold\n\n\"Pay now\" opens Stripe's hosted checkout\n\nNEW: A cart fully covered by welcome credit ($0 due) skips Stripe and lands on the success page\n\nOrder appears in Aguirre's order history after checkout\n\nUse Stripe test cards only during the pilot.\nCheckout is Stripe's real hosted page running in test mode. Don't enter a live card\nnumber — use a Stripe test card (e.g. 4242 4242 4242 4242). If you're ever unsure\nwhether you're on test mode, stop and report it.\n\nScenario 3b — Viewing order history\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\nOnce an order has been placed, Aguirre should be able to see it in her history —\nincluding delivered and cancelled orders that no longer show on the home dashboard.\n\n• From the home screen, tap View all → on the pending-orders card. (If there are no pending orders, use the View past orders → link in the empty-state card instead.)\n\n• You should land on /resident/order-bags/history with a list of every bag order, newest first.\n\n• Go back to Order bags and tap Past orders → in the top-right of that screen — it should take you to the same history page.\n\n• If Aguirre has no orders yet, the page should show an empty state with an Order bags → call-to-action.\n\nExpected: Each row shows the sheet/bag count, total charged, current\nstatus (Delivered / Queued / Out for delivery / Cancelled), the placed date, and either\nthe delivered date (if delivered) or the upcoming route date.\n\nVerify:\n\nHome dashboard → View all link opens the history page\n\nOrder Bags screen → Past orders link opens the history page\n\nOrders are sorted newest-first\n\nDelivered orders show a delivered date; pending/queued orders show an ETA or Delivery date TBD\n\nA resident with no orders sees the empty state (not a blank list)\n\nScenario 4 — Scanning a bag before pickup day\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre on her phone.\n\nPrerequisite: Aguirre needs at least one unused bag sticker in her account. The dev team\ncan pre-issue a sheet to her for testing, or Aguirre the operator will deliver one\nduring the operator guide.\n\n• Tap Scan on the bottom nav.\n\n• Allow camera access.\n\n• Hold the camera over the QR code on one of her stickers.\n\n• If the camera can't read the sticker, tap Enter code manually and type the printed number (something like BAG-4237-03 ).\n\n• Pick Separated or Mixed for what's inside the bag.\n\n• Take the \"before\" photo of the filled bag on her doorstep.\n\n• Submit.\n\nExpected: Aguirre sees a \"Your bag is scheduled for pickup\" confirmation\nand the bag now shows up in the Upcoming pickups area of her dashboard.\n\nVerify:\n\nQR scanner reads the sticker directly\n\nManual code entry works as a fallback\n\nPicking \"Separated\" vs \"Mixed\" is saved (visible in the pickup details)\n\nScenario 5 — Payout calculator\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Tap Calculator on the bottom nav.\n\n• Type weights into each material field (try 1 lb aluminum, 2 lb cardboard, etc.). Each input now shows a lbs suffix on the right (Apr 27 update).\n\n• Toggle Clean & separated on and off.\n\n• If admin has added a custom material (Phase 4 · Scenario 5b), confirm it appears in the input list with its custom name.\n\n• If admin has scheduled a pricing campaign (Phase 4 · Scenario 5c), confirm a 🔥 Bonus running now banner appears at the top of the page, and the affected material's row in Accepted materials shows a ×N tag with the boosted \"you get\" amount.\n\nExpected: The estimated points update live. Flipping the \"separated\"\ntoggle roughly doubles the estimate. Dollar equivalents ($10 per 1,000 pts)\nmatch the point totals. With a ×2 campaign on aluminum, the same 1 lb of aluminum should\naward double the points it did before.\n\nVerify:\n\nLive updates as you type\n\nSeparated multiplier behaves correctly\n\nNEW: Each input row shows the lbs suffix\n\nNEW: Custom materials added by admin appear here within seconds (no redeploy required)\n\nNEW: Live campaign multiplier compounds with the separated 2× — a 1 lb aluminum bag with separated + ×2 campaign earns ~4× the no-campaign mixed amount\n\nScenario 6 — Recycling guide\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Tap Guide on the bottom nav.\n\n• Scroll through the whole page.\n\nExpected: A readable explainer covering what each of the 7 accepted\nmaterials looks like, what counts as contamination, and how to prepare each type. No\nbroken images, no lorem ipsum.\n\nVerify:\n\nAll images render\n\nText is written for a homeowner, not for a developer\n\nScenario 7 — Rewards and redemption\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Tap Rewards on the bottom nav.\n\n• Confirm the current balance matches the home screen.\n\n• Look at the 4 redemption tiles: Amazon gift card , Walmart gift card , Pay trash bill , Donate to nonprofit .\n\n• Tap Amazon gift card .\n\n• Pick a dollar value (e.g. $5 = 500 pts).\n\n• Submit the request.\n\nExpected: Aguirre's points balance drops by the selected amount and a\nnew entry appears in her history (\"Amazon gift card — pending\"). The request will also\nshow up in Tia's admin redemption queue — you'll verify that in the Admin guide.\n\nPay trash bill and Donate tiles should be visible but\ndisabled (greyed out) with a \"Coming soon\" label. That's intentional for the pilot.\n\nRedemption is pilot-gated (NEW · Jun 9)\n\nEach resident can redeem once during the pilot, and the Redeem buttons\nlock with a friendly notice once the overall pilot cap is reached. A locked button is\nexpected behavior, not a bug.\n\nVerify:\n\nBalance decreases immediately\n\nHistory shows the pending redemption\n\nPay trash bill / Donate are disabled, not crash buttons\n\nNEW: After redeeming once (or when the pilot cap is hit), Redeem buttons lock with a friendly notice\n\nScenario 8 — Profile\n\nResident Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Open the account menu (top-right) → Profile .\n\n• Confirm name, email, phone, and address are correct.\n\n• Use the back-to-home header at the top of the page to return to the dashboard (Jun 9 addition).\n\n• Try editing the phone number and saving.\n\n• Find the Refer a friend section and try the personal referral link — copy it, share via SMS / email / X / Facebook, scan the QR code, and use the native share sheet (Jun 9 addition).\n\n• Try signing out from this screen.\n\nExpected: Editable fields save cleanly. The pickup day field\nis read-only (only admin can change it). The back-to-home header returns to the\ndashboard. Sign-out returns to the public homepage.\n\nVerify:\n\nPhone saves and persists after reload\n\nPickup day is display-only\n\nNEW: Back-to-home header returns to the dashboard\n\nNEW: Refer-a-friend link copies, shares (SMS/email/X/Facebook/native share), and shows a QR code\n\nWhen to stop and report\n\nIf any of these fail, note which scenario, what you did, what you expected, and what\nactually happened. Screenshots help. You don't need to re-test all of Phase 3 after a fix —\njust re-run the single scenario that failed.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 3 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Admin Dashboard Guide (phase-4-admin.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 4 · Admin Dashboard\n\nTia's back office: invites, zones, depots, material pricing, and the gift card queue.\nAll scenarios in this guide are run as Tia Johnson .\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nA few admin actions only simulate their external effect during the pilot. Treat\nthese as expected, not bugs:\n\n• Invitation emails — generated and written to Firestore, but the email itself is logged to the server console (no real inbox delivery yet).\n\n• Gift card redemptions — approving a request does not auto-send a gift card; an admin emails it manually after approval.\n\n• Material price changes are real and do affect live resident calculations — be deliberate when you edit prices during testing.\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• The overview dashboard shows real numbers, not placeholders.\n\n• Tia can send an operator invitation and it arrives (or simulates arriving).\n\n• Zones and depots can be created, edited, and deleted.\n\n• Material prices save and the resident calculator immediately reflects the change.\n\n• Gift card requests from residents appear in the redemption queue.\n\nScenario 1 — Overview dashboard\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• Sign in. You land on /admin .\n\n• Read every KPI tile and every chart.\n\nExpect to see:\n\n• Total residents — should include Aguirre and Goode once they've signed up.\n\n• Bags picked up this week / this month .\n\n• Material totals — pounds of aluminum, cardboard, etc.\n\n• Revenue — rough total value processed.\n\nExpected: Numbers are real (zero is fine — it means nothing has been\nprocessed yet). No \"TODO\" text, no sample data, no broken charts.\n\nPending-orders action item NEW · Jun 9\nThe dashboard flags paid bag orders awaiting route assignment as an\naction item, so you can see at a glance when an order has been paid for but not yet\nplaced on a route.\n\nVerify:\n\nResident count matches the signups you've done\n\nMaterial totals all read 0 before any depot processing\n\nScenario 2 — Invite an operator\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\nThis is how Operator Aguirre gets his operator account. Do this before\nyou run any operator scenarios.\n\n• In the sidebar, click Users & Invites .\n\n• Click Send invite .\n\n• Fill in Aguirre's name, email ( operator.aguirre@wbct-pilot.test ), and pick role Operator .\n\n• Click Send .\n\nExpected: The invite appears in the list below with status\npending . Tia can click it to copy the invite link (real email sending is\nsimulated during the pilot — the dev team can fetch the link for you if needed).\n\nVerify:\n\nInvite appears with Aguirre's email\n\nThe invite link can be copied\n\nOnce Aguirre accepts (see Phase 1, Scenario 5), the invite status flips to accepted\n\nTry the other roles too.\nRepeat the invite flow for a depot worker and a depot manager, even though there's no\napp flow built yet for those roles. You're just checking that the invite is issued and\nreceived. Name them Lisa Park (depot worker) and David Nguyen (depot\nmanager) for consistency in future guides.\n\nInvite signup needs a confirmed password NEW · Jun 9\nWhen the invitee accepts, the signup form now has password and\nconfirm password fields — they must match before the account is created.\n\nDeleting staff NEW · Jun 9\nYou can now delete operators, depot workers, and depot managers from the\nUsers screen (previously only residents). Deletion detaches active assignments (nulls a\ndepot's managerId / a route's operatorId ) while keeping\noperational history. You can't delete yourself or another admin.\n\nScenario 3 — Residents list\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• Open Users & Invites → Residents tab .\n\n• Find Resident Aguirre.\n\n• Click her row.\n\nExpected: A detail panel shows Aguirre's name, email, phone, address,\nzone, current points balance, and a history of her transactions (signup bonus,\npickups, redemptions).\n\nVerify:\n\nDetails match what Aguirre sees in her own profile\n\nTransaction history is chronological\n\nDeleting residents NEW · Jun 9\nThe Residents tab now supports per-row delete and bulk delete\n(tick the row checkboxes), each behind a confirmation modal. Deleting a resident\ncascade-wipes their data (addresses, bag orders, bags, transactions, redemptions), scrubs\nthem from routes and compliance batches, deletes their Stripe customer, and removes the\nauth account. There's no undo — use real care during testing.\n\nScenario 4 — Zones and depots\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• In the sidebar, click Zones & Depots .\n\n• Check that the pilot zone (e.g. \"North District\") already exists.\n\n• Create a second test zone called Testing Zone with any zip code.\n\n• Edit its name to Testing Zone 2 . Save.\n\n• Delete Testing Zone 2 .\n\n• Create a depot and assign it to the original pilot zone.\n\nExpected: Create / edit / delete all work and persist after reload.\nDeleting a zone that still has residents should be blocked with a clear message.\n\nVerify:\n\nCreate / edit / delete all persist across reloads\n\nDeleting a zone with residents is blocked\n\nAddress autocomplete NEW · Jun 9\nThe zone/depot address fields (and the commercial-account form) now use\nGoogle Places Autocomplete — a dark-theme variant of the same\nplace-picking UX residents get at signup. Start typing an address and pick from the\nsuggestions.\n\nScenario 4b — Per-depot accepted materials NEW · Apr 27\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\nEach depot can be configured to accept only a subset of materials (Kirk's example:\na Tocco Hills depot that doesn't take HDPE or mixed plastic).\n\n• Open Zones & Depots and find the pilot depot.\n\n• Below the address, locate the Accepted materials (X/7) chip row.\n\n• Click the HDPE chip to deselect it. The chip dims; a Save button appears.\n\n• Click Save . The page refreshes and the chip stays dim.\n\n• Now sign in as Depot Goode in another browser and open a pending bag in /depot/process .\n\n• Confirm the HDPE row is gone from the weight inputs.\n\n• Try to submit a bag with an HDPE weight via the API directly — it should return 400 material_not_accepted_hdpe .\n\n• Back as Tia, re-enable HDPE, save, and confirm Goode sees the row again.\n\nExpected: Depots that pre-date this feature (no acceptedMaterials\nfield) behave as if they accept everything — that's the legacy fallback.\nThe depot Inventory page also keeps showing materials that have been processed historically\neven if they were later removed from the accepted set.\n\nVerify:\n\nSaving requires at least one material — empty selection is blocked\n\nToggling a chip without saving doesn't persist (Reset link clears edits)\n\nWorker's process form rerenders without HDPE within seconds of save\n\nScenario 5 — Yellow-sheet pricing editor\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• In the sidebar, click Pricing .\n\n• You'll see 7 rows — one per material (aluminum, tin/steel, cardboard, paper, PET, HDPE, mixed plastic).\n\n• Each row has a market price ($/lb) and a customer payout % .\n\n• Change aluminum's market price to $1.00/lb . Save.\n\n• In a second browser, open Aguirre's account and go to her Calculator.\n\n• Enter 1 lb of aluminum.\n\nExpected: With a 30% payout, 1 lb should yield 30 points (= $0.30).\nDouble-check by flipping on Separated — points should roughly double to\n60.\n\nVerify:\n\nPrice change saves and persists on reload\n\nResident calculator reflects the new price within a few seconds\n\nA history of previous prices is visible (snapshot each time you save)\n\nScenario 5b — Add a custom material (Compost / Textile / Electronics) NEW · Apr 27\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\nThe seed yellow sheet ships with 7 materials. Admins can extend it on the fly when a\nnew commodity comes online (e.g. compost), or hide an existing one when its market\ncrashes. New materials are soft-deleted only — historical records\nstay intact.\n\n• Open Pricing . Scroll to the dashed + Add to yellow sheet composer below the table.\n\n• Type Compost for the name, leave $/lb at 0.00 , payout at 0 .\n\n• Click + Add to yellow sheet . A new row appears with id compost and a green new badge.\n\n• Click Save pricing . The badge clears; a priceHistory snapshot is written.\n\n• Now uncheck the Active box on the Mixed Plastic row to simulate a market crash. Save.\n\nExpected: Compost shows up on the resident calculator and on Goode's\nprocess form (assuming the depot has it in its accepted set — see Scenario 4b).\nMixed Plastic disappears from both the calculator and the process form. Historical\nmixed-plastic inventory and bagProcessing rows are still readable in admin reports.\n\nVerify:\n\nNames slugify correctly: \"Mixed Glass\" → mixed_glass , \"E-Waste\" → e_waste\n\nTrying to add a duplicate id is blocked client-side\n\nRe-checking Active on Mixed Plastic restores it without re-entering pricing\n\nTracked-only materials (compost / textile) with payout=0 award zero points but log inventory + landfill diversion\n\nScenario 5c — Pricing campaign (×2 on Aluminum) NEW · Apr 27\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\nFrom the meeting: \"if we know the price of aluminum is real high, can we send out alerts\nlike, aluminum is three times the points today?\" — yes. Pricing Campaigns apply a global\n×N multiplier to selected materials over a date range. Multipliers compound when multiple\ncampaigns overlap on the same material.\n\n• On the Pricing page, scroll past the yellow sheet to the Pricing campaigns section.\n\n• Name: Aluminum 2× week . Multiplier: 2 . Starts: today. Ends: 7 days out.\n\n• Toggle the Aluminum material chip on. Click Start campaign .\n\n• The new campaign appears in Active & upcoming with a LIVE badge.\n\n• In another browser, sign in as Aguirre. The dashboard now shows a 🔥 BONUS RUNNING NOW banner; the calculator's Aluminum row has a ×2 tag.\n\n• Sign in as Goode and open a pending bag — a 🔥 Active campaign banner appears above contamination, and the points preview applies the multiplier.\n\n• Submit a bag with 1 lb aluminum. Compare the awarded points against a control bag with no campaign — should be ~2× higher.\n\n• Click End now on the campaign row. Confirm the banners disappear in both browsers within seconds.\n\nExpected: Multiplier compounds correctly: schedule a ×1.5 on aluminum\non top of the live ×2 — preview should reflect ×3. The applied campaign array is\nsnapshotted onto the bagProcessing doc ( appliedCampaigns field)\nso historical points are auditable even after the campaign ends.\n\nVerify:\n\n\"End now\" sets active: false and pulls endsAt to now\n\nScheduling a campaign with end ≤ start is blocked\n\nCampaign with no materials selected is blocked\n\nResident dashboard banner only shows live campaigns (not future ones)\n\nScenario 6 — Redemption queue\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\nPrerequisite: Aguirre requested a gift card in Phase 3, Scenario 7. If you haven't done\nthat yet, do it now.\n\n• In the sidebar, click Redemptions .\n\n• Find Aguirre's pending Amazon gift card request.\n\n•\nPretend you've emailed her the gift card code out of band (during the pilot we do this\nmanually).\n\n• Click Mark fulfilled .\n\nExpected: The request moves from Pending to\nFulfilled . In Aguirre's own Rewards history, the entry changes status to\nFulfilled too.\n\nVerify:\n\nStatus flips on both sides (admin queue and Aguirre's history)\n\nPoints are not refunded when fulfilling (they were already deducted)\n\nPilot budget gate NEW · Jun 9\nThe Redemptions page shows a budget tile (e.g. N/100 claimed,\n$X/$1000 committed ). During the pilot each resident is capped at\none redemption (1,000 points = $10) — the API rejects any over-cap request.\n\nScenario 7 — Admin Assistant (AI help chat) NEW · Jun 9\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\nA new Assistant item (💬) in the admin nav opens\n/admin/assistant — an AI chat that answers questions about how the app\nworks for every role, grounded only in these user guides. It's admin-only.\n\n• In the sidebar, click Assistant .\n\n• Click a suggestion button (e.g. \"How does a resident earn points?\" ) or type your own question.\n\n• Watch the answer stream in.\n\nExpected: Answers are drawn from the user guides. If no\nGEMINI_API_KEY is set, the chat runs in demo mode (an amber\n\"Demo mode\" notice appears); set GEMINI_API_KEY for live answers.\n\nVerify:\n\nSuggestion buttons populate and send a question\n\nAnswers stream in rather than appearing all at once\n\nA non-admin can't reach /admin/assistant\n\nScenario 8 — Orders this week NEW · Jun 9\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• Open the Orders this week screen.\n\n• Look at the orders grouped under each route.\n\nExpected: Within each route group, orders are sorted\nnewest-first and show a Date column (e.g.\n\"Mon, May 27\" ). Queued / unrouted orders appear in their own separate section.\n\nVerify:\n\nNewest order in each group is at the top\n\nDate column reads correctly per order\n\nQueued/unrouted orders are listed separately\n\nAdmin sidebar at a glance\n\nMenu item\nWhat Tia does there\n\nOverview\nKPI dashboard. Read-only.\n\nUsers & Invites\nList residents, send invitations to employees.\n\nZones & Depots\nDefine service areas and drop-off locations.\n\nPricing\nEdit material prices + payout %, add new materials, hide retired ones, and schedule global ×N pricing campaigns.\n\nRedemptions\nApprove gift card requests one at a time.\n\nRoutes\n\nBuild the daily pickup route and assign it to an operator (covered in the\nRoutes guide — Phase 5).\n\nAssistant 💬 NEW · Jun 9\nAI help chat grounded in the user guides; answers how-the-app-works questions for every role. Admin-only.\n\nSign a non-admin account in and try to reach /admin.\nOpen a private window, sign in as Aguirre, and manually type /admin into the\nURL. You should be bounced back to the resident home. If the admin dashboard loads for a\nresident, that's a serious bug — stop and report.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 4 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Route Builder Guide (phase-5-routes.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 5 · Building a Pickup Route\n\nHow Tia turns \"these residents need a pickup\" into a driving plan for Aguirre. This is\nthe bridge between the admin dashboard (Phase 4) and the operator app (Phase 6) — you\nneed to run this one before operator testing will work.\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\n\n• Operator assignment notification — Aguirre sees the route when he opens the app; no SMS or push is sent for the pilot.\n\n• The Google Maps Routes API call itself is not stubbed — it runs for real, so you'll see genuine optimized ordering.\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• Tia can pull up pending pickups and bag-order deliveries for a zone.\n\n• The route optimizer returns an ordered list of stops.\n\n• Tia can assign the route to an operator by name.\n\n• When a resident places a bag order, it gets enqueued onto the next open route for that zone.\n\n📋 Before you start\nDevice: 💻 Laptop (you're Tia for this whole guide).\n\nDependencies — tick each before running Scenario 1:\n\n• Phase 4 — Tia has invited an operator (Aguirre) and he has accepted.\n\n• Phase 3 · Scenario 4 — at least one resident has scanned a bag for pickup (Aguirre).\n\n• Phase 3 · Scenario 3 — a second resident has a pending bag order (Goode).\n\nSkipping any of these leaves the route empty — the builder has nothing to schedule.\n\nScenario 1 — Build tomorrow's route\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• In the admin sidebar, click Routes .\n\n• Click New route .\n\n• Pick tomorrow's date.\n\n• Pick the zone Aguirre and Goode live in.\n\n• Pick a delivery time window for the run (e.g. 9am–noon).\n\n• Click Pull pending stops .\n\nExpected: Both Aguirre and Goode appear in the stop list. Aguirre has a\npickup (her scanned bag). Goode has a delivery (his bag order). Each stop shows the\naddress and what's happening there.\n\nDelivery time window + resident notifications NEW · Jun 9\n\nBuilding a route now requires picking a delivery time window . On save,\nevery resident on the route is notified of that window by SMS and email\n(SMS is the console stub for the pilot; email goes out for real), and the window shows\nup on each resident's dashboard as their scheduled delivery slot.\n\nVerify:\n\nBoth residents appear in the stop list\n\nPickups and deliveries are visually distinguished\n\nA delivery time window must be chosen before the route can be built\n\nScenario 2 — Optimize the stop order\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• With the stop list showing, click Optimize order .\n\n• Wait a moment.\n\nExpected: The stops get reordered into a sensible driving sequence. A\nsmall map preview shows the resulting path. The total estimated drive time and\ndistance show at the bottom of the list.\n\nVerify:\n\nStop order changes after clicking Optimize\n\nMap preview matches the new order\n\nScenario 3 — Assign and save\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• In the Operator field, pick Operator Aguirre .\n\n• Click Save route .\n\nExpected: The route appears in the routes list with status\nscheduled , tomorrow's date, and Aguirre's name. If you sign in as Aguirre (on a\ndifferent device or private window) he should see the route on his operator home\nscreen. On save, each resident on the route is also notified of the delivery window by\nSMS + email, and that window now appears on their dashboard.\n\nVerify:\n\nRoute saves and appears in list\n\nAguirre can see the route after the save\n\nEach resident's dashboard shows the scheduled delivery window after save\n\nAn email (and SMS stub log) goes out per resident with the window\n\nScenario 4 — Late bag order joins the route\n\nThis scenario tests that an order placed after the route is built still gets\npicked up, by being tacked onto the next open route.\n\nResident Signed in as Resident Goode on his phone.\n\n• Have Goode order another sheet of bags (repeat Phase 3, Scenario 3).\n\n• Now sign back in as Tia and reopen tomorrow's route.\n\nExpected: Goode's new order has automatically been added to the existing\nroute's delivery list — Tia doesn't have to click anything. The stop count goes up by\none if Goode wasn't already on the route, or the existing stop now shows the additional\ndelivery.\n\nVerify:\n\nNew order attaches to the open route without admin action\n\nScenario 5 — Edit a route\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia Johnson.\n\n• Open an existing scheduled route.\n\n• Remove a stop manually using its × button.\n\n• Re-run Optimize order .\n\n• Save.\n\nExpected: The edit sticks. If Aguirre was viewing the route at the same\ntime he should see it update on the next reload.\n\nVerify:\n\nRemoving a stop works\n\nRe-optimization doesn't bring the removed stop back\n\nThings to try that should fail\n\n•\nTrying to save a route with no operator assigned — the save button should be\ndisabled or the form should block you.\n\n•\nBuilding two routes for the same date, same zone, same operator — you should get a\nwarning or a clean error.\n\n•\nPicking a date in the past — not allowed.\n\nIf the map doesn't load.\nThe route builder uses Google Maps. If the map tile is blank or the optimize button errors\nout with a Google-related message, the Maps API key may not be configured — flag it to the\ndev team, don't keep retrying.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 5 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Operator Day Guide (phase-6-operator.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 6 · Running an Operator Day\n\nWhat the driver does from the moment they open the app in the morning to the moment they\nreturn to the depot. All scenarios in this guide are run as\nOperator Aguirre on a phone browser, with Tia and\nAguirre on the side to set things up and verify.\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nA couple of operator-facing touches don't go all the way through real services during the\npilot:\n\n• \"On my way\" texts to residents are logged to the server console, not sent by SMS. You trigger them from the app; the receiving phone doesn't actually buzz.\n\n• Driver-on-the-way is tap-triggered by the operator, not auto-fired from GPS geofence.\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• Aguirre sees today's route when Tia has assigned it to him.\n\n• He can start the route, work through stops, and close it out.\n\n• Each stop's QR scan matches the expected resident — scanning the wrong bag is rejected.\n\n• \"Skip\", \"Not out\", and \"Issue\" all behave differently.\n\n• Delivering a sheet of new bags to a resident registers the 10 bags in the resident's account.\n\n• After the route is closed, the resident's bag shows up in the depot's incoming list (will be checked when Phase 7 lands).\n\n📋 Before you start\nDevice: 📱 Phone (primary, for Aguirre) + 💻 Laptop on the side (Tia, to set things up and verify).\n\nDependencies — tick each before running Scenario 1:\n\n• Phase 5 — Tia has built today's route and assigned it to operator Aguirre.\n\n• The route has at least one pickup stop (a resident with a scanned bag) and one delivery stop (a resident with a pending bag order).\n\nScenario 1 — Seeing today's route\n\nOperator Signed in as Operator Aguirre on a phone.\n\n• Open the site on the phone. Sign in as Aguirre.\n\n• Land on the operator home screen.\n\nExpected: The screen shows a summary row at the top\n( Stops done / total , Bags , Deliveries ), a\n\"Ready to roll?\" card asking Aguirre to start the route, and a preview of the first\nstop.\n\nShared wordmark header NEW · Jun 9\n\nThe operator home now opens with a white We Buy Clean Trash. wordmark\nbar pinned flush to the very top edge of the screen (with safe-area padding so it sits\nbelow the notch on iPhones). The avatar and sign-out live inside this bar. It's the same\nheader now shared across the resident, operator, and depot apps.\n\nVerify:\n\nThe operator home screen loads without needing admin menus\n\nAguirre sees Aguirre's and Goode's addresses — not anyone else's\n\nIf no route is scheduled, the screen says \"No active route\" (not an error)\n\nScenario 2 — Start route and tap \"Driver on the way\"\n\nOperator Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Tap ▶ Start route .\n\n• Once the first stop's card is showing, tap 📲 Text resident: driver on the way .\n\nExpected: The \"Ready to roll?\" card disappears and the current stop\ncard becomes interactive. The \"on the way\" button fires instantly and shows a\nconfirmation (\"Text sent\" or similar).\n\nDuring the pilot the SMS isn't actually sent — it's simulated. You don't need to\ncheck Aguirre's phone. The test is just that the button responds.\n\nVerify:\n\nStart Route transitions the screen cleanly\n\n\"On the way\" button shows a confirmation toast\n\nScenario 3 — Complete a pickup\n\nOperator Signed in as Aguirre.\n\nFor this test, the \"resident\" can just be a printed QR on a table — you don't have to\ndrive anywhere.\n\n• On the current stop (Aguirre's house), tap ✓ Scan & complete .\n\n• Allow camera access. Point the camera at Aguirre's pre-scanned bag QR sticker.\n\n•\nIf the camera is being stubborn, tap Enter code manually and type the\nprinted bag number (e.g. BAG-4237-03 ).\n\n• Leave Bag sealed properly on (green).\n\n• Leave Visible contamination off (grey).\n\n• Take a quick photo (any photo is fine — it's the \"I was here\" record).\n\n• Tap Confirm pickup .\n\nExpected: A checkmark flashes. Aguirre is returned to today's route,\nwhich now shows \"1 of 2 stops done\" (or similar) and moves the current-stop card to\nthe next address.\n\nVerify:\n\nThe wrong QR is rejected — try scanning a random QR code (from anywhere) and it should refuse\n\nThe pickup counter increments on the home screen\n\nIf Aguirre had 3 bags, Aguirre has to repeat this 3 times — one per bag\n\nScenario 4 — Skip vs Not Out vs Issue\n\nOperator Signed in as Aguirre.\n\nFor this one, it's easiest to set up a third test resident you can abuse. If you don't\nhave one, just reuse Goode — do the Skip test first, then come back and Not-out him.\n\nSkip\n\n• Tap ⏭ Skip on Goode's stop.\n\n• Continue with the other stops.\n\n• Reload the page.\n\nExpected: After reload, Goode's stop reappears at the end of the list.\nSkipping is session-only — useful for \"I'll come back to this one later today.\"\n\nNot out\n\n• Tap 📦 Not out on Goode's stop.\n\nExpected: The stop is marked missed permanently. It does not\ncome back on reload. Aguirre moves on.\n\nIssue\n\n• Set up a fourth test stop (or use any stop you haven't completed).\n\n• Tap ⚠ Issue .\n\n• Pick Contaminated , type a short note like \"Bag full of food waste.\"\n\n• Submit.\n\nExpected: The stop is flagged as having an issue with Aguirre's note.\nTia can see the note later in the route summary / admin view.\n\nVerify:\n\nSkip is session-only\n\nNot out persists across reload\n\nIssue records the note\n\nScenario 5 — Deliver a fresh sticker sheet\n\nOperator Signed in as Aguirre.\n\nGoode ordered bags in Phase 5. Today Aguirre is delivering them. In real life Aguirre would\ncarry printed sticker sheets of 10 QRs each; for testing, the dev team will give you\none printed sheet per test order, or you can use a dummy QR sheet.\n\n• At Goode's stop, look for the green Deliver bags + stickers sub-card.\n\n• Tap it.\n\n• Scan the first sticker on a fresh sheet — the one with the lowest printed number (ending in -01 ).\n\nExpected: The app claims all 10 stickers for Goode at once. Goode's\naccount now has 10 unused bag stickers available. If you sign in as Goode and go to\nScan , he should be able to scan any of those 10 bags.\n\nVerify:\n\nScanning the first sticker succeeds\n\nScanning a middle sticker (e.g. -05) is rejected with a helpful message\n\nGoode's unused-bag count goes up by 10\n\nScenario 6 — End of day\n\nOperator Signed in as Aguirre.\n\n• Work through the rest of the stops (complete, not out, or issue — each stop needs some outcome).\n\n• Once everything has an outcome, the home screen replaces the current-stop card with All stops handled ✓ .\n\n• Tap End-of-route summary .\n\n• Read the numbers — Stops visited, Bags picked up, Missed, Issues, Deliveries fulfilled.\n\n• Scroll to the bottom and tap Deliver to depot .\n\nExpected: The route flips to completed . It disappears from\nAguirre's home (tomorrow's route will replace it when built). Tia can see the\ncompleted route with all its outcomes in the admin routes list.\n\nVerify:\n\nSummary numbers match what Aguirre actually did\n\n\"Deliver to depot\" is a one-way confirmation\n\nAfter closing, Aguirre cannot edit stops\n\nOnce closed, it's closed.\nDeliberately try to re-open the route after tapping Deliver to depot. Aguirre should not\nbe able to. If he can, that's a bug.\n\nQuick troubleshooting\n\nWhat you see\nLikely cause\nWhat to try\n\n\"No active route\"\nTia didn't assign a route to Aguirre for today, or assigned it for the wrong date.\nHave Tia check /admin/routes .\n\nCamera stays black\nBrowser blocked camera access.\nReload, grant permission, or use manual code entry.\n\nScan keeps saying \"wrong bag\"\nThe QR you're scanning isn't assigned to this address.\nDouble-check you're at the right stop. Try manual entry. If you're sure, flag as an Issue.\n\nStart route button does nothing\nRoute is already started on another device.\nReload — the current stop card should already be showing.\n\nWhat's not testable yet\n\nAguirre finishing a route is meant to hand the pickups over to a depot worker for weighing\nand points awarding. That depot flow is Phase 7, which isn't built yet — so after \"Deliver\nto depot,\" nothing further happens for the resident. Aguirre won't see points for her pickup\nuntil Phase 7 is in. That's expected for this build.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 6 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Depot Day Guide (phase-7-depot.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 7 · Running a Depot Day\n\nWhat the depot worker does from the moment a route arrives to the moment the resident's\npoints balance ticks up. All scenarios are run as Depot Goode on a tablet\nor laptop, with Tia , Aguirre , and Aguirre\non the side to set things up and verify.\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nThe depot flow itself is real — points, ledger, and inventory all update for real. The\nonly touches that are stubbed:\n\n• Weight entry is manual. No Bluetooth or USB scale integration — weigh on your scale, type the number in.\n\n• \"Bag processed\" SMS to the resident is logged to the server console, not sent by text.\n\nWhat you're verifying (happy path, ~15 min)\n\n• Goode gets invited and lands on her depot's Incoming screen.\n\n• Aguirre's completed routes show up under \"Incoming deliveries.\"\n\n• Scanning a bag QR — or typing the printed number — jumps to the process form.\n\n•\nSubmitting the form awards points to Aguirre, appends a transaction ledger entry, marks the\nbag processed, and adds weight to the depot inventory — all in one atomic write.\n\nScenarios 1–4 below cover the happy path. Everything after is optional\nadvanced testing — edge cases most testers can skip.\n\n📋 Before you start\nDevice: 💻 Laptop or tablet (Goode) + 📱 Phone (Aguirre, for a prior pickup) + 💻 Laptop on the side (Tia, to verify).\n\nDependencies — tick each before running Scenario 1:\n\n• Phase 4 — Yellow Sheet prices set for all 7 materials.\n\n• Phase 4 — a Depot exists that Tia can link Goode to.\n\n• Phase 5 — a route built for Aguirre that includes a pickup for Aguirre.\n\n• Phase 6 · Scenario 6 — Aguirre completed the route and tapped Deliver to depot .\n\nIf any of those are missing, Goode's Incoming screen will be empty.\n\nScenario 1 — Invite the depot worker\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia on a laptop.\n\n• Open /admin/invites .\n\n• Click New invite .\n\n• Enter Goode's email, pick role Depot worker .\n\n•\nA Depot dropdown appears once the role is set. Pick the pilot depot\n(the one Tia created in Phase 4 — e.g. \"Depot #01 — Ohio West\").\n\n• Send the invite.\n\nExpected: An invite link is generated. Tia copies it and sends it\nto Goode. When Goode opens the link, sets a password, and lands in the app, her user\nrecord now has both the depot_worker role and the depotId pointing at\nthe pilot depot.\n\nVerify:\n\nThe Depot dropdown only appears for depot-worker / depot-manager invites\n\nGoode lands at /depot/incoming after accepting\n\nThe depot name in the header matches the depot Tia picked\n\nScenario 2 — Seeing incoming deliveries\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode on a tablet.\n\nFor this scenario you need a route Aguirre has already closed out (Phase 6, Scenario 6).\nIf he hasn't, jump back and finish that first.\n\n• Open the app and sign in as Goode.\n\n• Land on the depot home, which redirects to /depot/incoming .\n\nExpected: A single card for Aguirre's route, labelled\nNEW . The row shows Aguirre's name, the number of bags he actually picked\nup, and the arrival time (whenever he tapped Deliver to depot ).\n\nShared header & yellow stats hero NEW · Jun 9\n\nThe depot app now opens with the same white We Buy Clean Trash.\nwordmark bar shared with the resident and operator apps — the avatar in it links to the\ndepot account page. The incoming stats hero now uses the unified yellow-header style\n(matching the operator app) rather than its old standalone look.\n\nDelivery-only routes are hidden NEW · Jun 9\n\nIncoming now skips delivery-only routes — runs where Aguirre only\ndropped off empty bag sheets and picked nothing up. With nothing to weigh or process,\nthey no longer clutter the list. A route shows here only when it carries at least one\nbag to process.\n\nVerify:\n\nMissed / issue bags are not counted in the bag total\n\nRoutes from a zone that isn't this depot's don't appear\n\nA delivery-only route (drop-offs, zero pickups) does not appear in Incoming\n\nIf nothing has arrived yet, the screen shows a friendly empty-state, not an error\n\nScenario 3 — Scan a bag into the process form\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode.\n\n• From Incoming, tap Aguirre's route row.\n\n• The route-detail screen lists every picked-up bag with a PENDING badge.\n\n• Tap 📷 Scan a bag .\n\n• Allow camera access. Point the camera at one of Aguirre's bag QR stickers.\n\n•\nIf the camera is being stubborn, tap the Or type the printed number field and\ntype the printed number (e.g. 0473 ) or the full code (e.g.\nBAG-4237-03 ). Case-insensitive.\n\nExpected: On a match, the app navigates to\n/depot/process/[bagId] . The header shows Aguirre's name, the printed\nnumber, and the bag's declared type (\"Commingled\" or \"Separated · 2× multiplier\").\n\nVerify:\n\nScanning a random QR (not from this route) is rejected with a clear message\n\nTapping a bag row directly — without scanning — also opens the form\n\nCanceling the scanner returns to the bag list without errors\n\nScenario 4 — Process a clean bag\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode.\n\nFor this test, the \"bag\" can be seven little piles of paper on Goode's table. The goal\nis that the numbers she types turn into points for Aguirre.\n\n• Leave Contamination on None (green).\n\n•\nType weights in each of the seven material rows. Suggested starter values:\n\n• 🥫 Aluminum — 1.4\n\n• 🧴 PET — 2.1\n\n• 📦 Cardboard — 0.6\n\n• Leave the other four at zero.\n\n• Watch the Total · X lbs · +Y points card recompute as you type.\n\n• Tap ✓ Submit & next bag .\n\nExpected: A green \"points awarded\" flash, then Goode bounces back to\nthe route bag list. The bag she just processed now reads PROCESSED .\nSign in as Aguirre in another browser — her points balance has gone up by exactly the\namount Goode saw on submit.\n\n⚙️ Dynamic accepted materials (NEW · Apr 27)\n\nThe 7 material rows you see on this form are not hardcoded anymore.\nThey come from the materials Firestore collection (active=true) intersected\nwith this depot's acceptedMaterials set (configured by admin in Zones &\nDepots — see Phase 4 Scenario 4b). If admin adds a new material like\nCompost and toggles it on for this depot, a new weight row appears here.\n\nVerify:\n\nThe preview total matches what Aguirre sees after submit\n\nAguirre's latest transaction row (on her rewards / profile screen) shows type pickup with a positive delta\n\nThe bag's status on the route list flipped to Processed on reload\n\nNEW: If admin removes a material from this depot's accepted set (Phase 4 · 4b), the row disappears here within seconds\n\nScenario 4b — Active pricing campaign banner NEW · Apr 27\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode, with admin Tia having scheduled a campaign.\n\nWhen admin runs a global pricing campaign (e.g. Aluminum 2× week , see\nPhase 4 · Scenario 5c ), the worker sees a\n🔥 Active campaign banner above the contamination row, and the live\npoints preview reflects the multiplier.\n\n• Have Tia start a campaign: ×2 on Aluminum for the next 7 days.\n\n• As Goode, refresh the process form for any pending bag. Confirm the orange banner reads ×2 on Aluminum · Aluminum 2× week .\n\n• Type 1.0 lb of aluminum. The preview points should be roughly double what the same input gave before the campaign.\n\n• Submit. Open the resulting bagProcessing doc in Firebase console and confirm appliedCampaigns contains the campaign id, name, multiplier, and material ids.\n\nExpected: Multipliers compound when more than one campaign overlaps —\nschedule a second ×1.5 campaign on aluminum and the preview should reflect ×3.\n\nVerify:\n\nBanner only shows campaigns whose start date has passed (scheduled-for-future ones don't appear here)\n\nThe preview multiplier applies per material — non-aluminum rows are unaffected\n\nWhen admin clicks \"End now\" on the campaign, refreshing the form clears the banner and the preview reverts\n\nOptional\n\nAdvanced — edge cases\n\nSkip this section unless you're debugging a specific behavior. The scenarios below cover\ncontamination penalties, the 2× separated-bag multiplier, re-processing guards, SMS stub\noutput, and the Inventory dashboard. None are required to sign off on Phase 7 — the happy\npath above is the release gate.\n\nScenario 5 — Contamination changes the math\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode.\n\nOpen a different pending bag and type the same weights as Scenario 4. Before submitting,\ntry each contamination level and watch the preview.\n\n•\nWith weights entered, tap None , Minor ,\nMajor , then Severe in turn.\n\n• Note the preview point count at each.\n\n• Finally leave it on Severe and submit.\n\nExpected: The four previews are roughly 100%, 70%, 40%, and 10% of\nthe \"None\" amount — because the penalties are 0 / 30 / 60 / 90%. The submitted severe\nbag awards Aguirre only ~10% of what the equivalent clean bag did.\n\nVerify:\n\nThe four preview amounts step down correctly\n\nSubmitting a severe bag is allowed (it's not a block — just a big penalty)\n\nThe contaminationSeverity is preserved on the bagProcessing record (admin view, when Phase 9 lands)\n\nScenario 6 — Separated bags get the 2× multiplier\n\nAdmin + Resident + Depot All three.\n\nThis scenario needs a bag that Aguirre declared as separated . If the Phase 6\ntest data didn't cover that, set one up now:\n\n•\nAs Aguirre, scan or declare a new bag with the Separated option (this is the\npre-pickup scan flow). If the UI doesn't expose the toggle, ask the dev team to set\nbags.declaredType = 'separated' on a test bag.\n\n• As Aguirre, run that bag through a pickup on today's route.\n\n• As Aguirre, close the route and deliver to depot.\n\n• As Goode, open the bag in the process form.\n\nExpected: The header on the process form reads \"Declared · Separated\n(2× multiplier)\". The live points preview is double what the same weights would be for\na commingled bag at the same contamination level.\n\nVerify:\n\nSeparated bags show the 2× label in the form header\n\nPreview is roughly 2× the matching commingled bag\n\nScenario 7 — Re-processing is refused\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode.\n\n• From the route bag list, try to tap an already- PROCESSED row.\n\n• Then open the scanner and try to scan that same processed bag.\n\nExpected: The row is disabled (greyed out, no click). The scanner\nshows \"Bag #<number> is already processed\" and drops back to the list instead of\nopening the form.\n\nVerify:\n\nProcessed rows can't be reopened\n\nScanning a processed QR flashes a message, not the form\n\nScenario 8 — Route progress fills in\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode.\n\n• Process a couple of bags from Aguirre's route but leave at least one still pending.\n\n• Tap ← Back to return to Incoming.\n\nExpected: The route's badge has moved from NEW to\nIN PROGRESS , a thin green bar shows how far along Goode is, and the\nsubtitle reads \"Processing X/Y\". After the last bag is processed, the badge flips to\nDONE .\n\nVerify:\n\nBadge transitions NEW → IN PROGRESS → DONE based on bag status\n\nProgress bar width matches the X/Y\n\nScenario 9 — Reading the Inventory dashboard\n\nDepot Signed in as Goode.\n\n• Tap Inventory in the top nav.\n\n• Review the Current stock (lbs) card for each of the seven materials.\n\n• Review the Yellow sheet (this month) card.\n\nExpected:\n\n•\nThe weights reflect exactly what Goode has submitted so far, summed per material\n(aluminum = 1.4 × number of clean bags with 1.4 lb of aluminum, etc.).\n\n•\nEach row has a progress bar that turns amber near 80% and red at 95% of the pilot\ncapacity (1,500 lbs per material). If any material hits 95%, a red alert banner\nappears above the cards.\n\n•\nThe Yellow Sheet lists each material's current market price per pound (not the\ncustomer share; the split stays admin-side).\n\nVerify:\n\nStock numbers match Goode's submissions\n\nBars visibly change color at the amber and red thresholds\n\nYellow sheet values match what Tia set in /admin/pricing\n\nScenario 10 — SMS notification stub fires\n\nTester With access to the dev console.\n\nTwilio isn't wired for the pilot, so Aguirre won't see a real text. But the app should\nstill log the intent so the post-pilot Twilio swap has something to hook into.\n\n• Watch the dev-server terminal while Goode submits a bag.\n\n•\nAfter submit, check the smsLog collection (Firestore console or a dev query).\n\nExpected: A console line like\n[sms-stub] to=... purpose=bag_processed body=Your bag #0473 was processed. +218 points added to your balance.\nplus a new smsLog document with the same purpose and a\nrelatedDocId pointing at the bagProcessing record just created.\n\nVerify:\n\nOne stub log per successful submit\n\nFailed submits (e.g., network error) do not leave a stub log behind\n\nQuick troubleshooting\n\nWhat you see\nLikely cause\nWhat to try\n\n\"No depot is assigned to your account\"\nGoode's invite didn't include a depot, or no depot exists yet.\n\nHave Tia re-invite Goode with the Depot dropdown set, or create a depot first in\n/admin/depots .\n\nIncoming is empty but Aguirre finished a route\nAguirre didn't tap Deliver to depot , or the route's zone isn't mapped to this depot.\n\nAsk Aguirre to re-open the app — the summary page should show an \"All stops handled\"\ncard with the Deliver to depot button. Or have Tia check zone↔depot\nmapping in /admin/depots .\n\nCamera stays black\nBrowser blocked camera access (especially on desktop Safari).\nReload and grant permission. Manual entry is always available.\n\nPreview points stay at 0\nAll weight fields are empty or zero.\nType a non-zero weight in any row — even 0.1 lb unlocks the form.\n\nSubmit button greyed out\nTotal weight is 0, or the form is already submitting.\nWait for the spinner, or add at least one weight.\n\nAguirre says she didn't get points\n\nTwo possible causes: (1) Goode's submit threw an error (check for a red banner),\n(2) Aguirre is looking at a cached dashboard.\n\nHave Aguirre reload her rewards / profile page. If still missing, check Firestore for a transactions doc with type=pickup and her uid.\n\nWhat's not testable yet\n\nDepot manager screens (Phase 8) are where mill pickups subtract from inventory —\nuntil that ships, inventory only grows. Contamination strike tracking (Phase 9) will\ngenerate warning letters for repeat offenders based on the severity flags Goode sets here;\nfor now the flags just sit on the bagProcessing records. Real Twilio SMS ships\npost-pilot. None of those gaps should block Phase 7 verification.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 7 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Phase 9 Admin Polish Guide (phase-9-admin-polish.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nPhase 9 · Admin polish\n\nWhat Tia sees after Phase 8 lands: three new panels on the admin dashboard (zone\nperformance, contamination alerts, operator leaderboard) and a new\nCompliance section for printing and mailing required residential\nnotices. Everything is admin-only — sign in as Tia on a laptop.\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nThe metrics are real; the mailing step is not automated:\n\n• Compliance notices — the app generates the PDF batch. An admin then prints, stuffs, and mails them by hand, and clicks \"Mark as mailed.\" No postage/automation integration during the pilot.\n\nNew admin polish since this guide shipped (NEW · Jun 9)\n\n• Admin Assistant — a new Assistant item in the sidebar opens\n/admin/assistant , an AI chat grounded in the user guides. Admin-only;\nruns in demo mode (canned answers) when GEMINI_API_KEY isn't set.\n\n• Delete staff & residents — the /admin/users screen\ncan now delete operators, depot workers, and managers, and delete residents one at a\ntime or in bulk, each behind a confirmation step.\n\n• Gift-card pilot gate — the /admin/redemptions page\nshows a budget tile (e.g. N/100 claimed · $X/$1,000 committed ), and residents\nare capped at one redemption during the pilot.\n\n• Pending-orders action item — the dashboard surfaces a pending bag\norders action item so Tia can clear the queue at a glance.\n\nWhat you're verifying\n\n• The dashboard now shows a Zone performance table with per-zone residents, bags, weight, and revenue for the current month.\n\n• Contamination alerts list the residents with the most flagged bags over the last 6 months, labelled 1st warning / 2nd strike / 3rd+ strike.\n\n• The Operator leaderboard ranks operators this month by completed pickups, with an issues tie-breaker.\n\n• Tia can generate an Initial Service Notice and a Semi-Annual Update batch per zone, print one letter per resident, and mark the batch as mailed.\n\n• Mailed batches become an auditable record in Firestore ( complianceBatches ) with status, generated-by, mailed-by, and timestamps.\n\n📋 Before you start\nDevice: 💻 Laptop (you're Tia for this whole guide).\n\nDependencies — these panels are data-driven, so earlier phases need to have run:\n\n• Phase 4 — 1 zone with 1 depot linked, plus 2–3 residents with addresses in the zone.\n\n• Phase 6 — at least one completed route.\n\n• Phase 7 — several processed bags, including at least a couple flagged Minor , Major , or Severe contamination (see Phase 7 Advanced · Scenario 5).\n\nScenario 1 — Read the zone performance table\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia on a laptop.\n\n• Open /admin .\n\n• Scroll below the KPI tiles to Zone performance .\n\n•\nFind the zone you've been testing in. Cross-check each column:\n\n• Residents should match the number of resident users Tia can count in /admin/users whose zone is this one.\n\n• Bags is the count of bagProcessing records for residents in this zone, this month.\n\n• Weight is the sum of every weight field on those bagProcessing records.\n\n• Revenue is the sum of bagOrders.total for the zone, this month, excluding cancelled orders.\n\nExpected: Each zone row reflects exactly what Goode weighed this\nmonth, not cumulative totals. A brand-new month shows zero bags and zero revenue even\nif the zone has lots of historical activity.\n\nVerify:\n\nZones with no resident activity still list (with zeros), not missing\n\nA resident without a zoneId does not show up in any row\n\nRevenue of a cancelled bag order does not appear\n\nScenario 2 — First-warning contamination alert\n\nDepot + Admin Two tabs.\n\n• As Goode, process one of Aguirre's bags with contamination set to Minor . Submit.\n\n• As Tia, reload /admin .\n\n• Scroll to the Contamination alerts panel.\n\nExpected: A row appears for Aguirre with the label\n1st warning , the gray WARNED badge, her zone name, the\nseverity (Minor), and the date Goode just submitted.\n\nVerify:\n\nA bag submitted with severity None does not add to the alerts list\n\nThe address / zone shown matches Aguirre's current address\n\nScenario 3 — Escalating to 2nd strike, then 3rd\n\nDepot + Admin Same two tabs.\n\n• As Goode, process a second Aguirre bag — Major contamination.\n\n• As Tia, reload /admin . Aguirre's row should now read 2nd strike with an amber STRIKE badge.\n\n• As Goode, process a third Aguirre bag — Severe .\n\n• As Tia, reload again. The row flips to 3rd strike with a red ACTION badge.\n\nExpected: The strike count is the total number of non-clean\nbagProcessing records for this resident across the last 6 months, regardless\nof severity. Severe does not count for more than Minor — the severity is only shown as\nthe \"last\" value for context.\n\nVerify:\n\nBadge color matches the count (gray at 1, amber at 2, red at 3+)\n\nA fourth flagged bag shows 4th strike (still red ACTION)\n\nMost-struck residents sort to the top\n\nScenario 4 — Operator leaderboard\n\nOperator + Admin Two tabs.\n\nFor this scenario the leaderboard only reads pickups.status = 'completed' (and\ncounts issue / skipped / missed into the issues column). You\nonly need one operator active to see data — more makes the ranking obvious.\n\n• As Aguirre the operator, complete a couple of pickups for today's route (Phase 6).\n\n• On the same route, tap Not out or Issue on one stop to generate an issue count.\n\n• As Tia, reload /admin and look at Operator leaderboard .\n\nExpected: Aguirre's name appears with a 🥇 medal icon, his completed\npickup count, and \"1 issue\" (or however many Not-Out/Issue/Skipped actions he did\ntoday). The list is capped at top 5.\n\nVerify:\n\nCompleted pickups from before the 1st of this month are not counted\n\nIf two operators tie on completions, the one with fewer issues ranks higher\n\nPickups that are still pending don't affect either column\n\nScenario 5 — Generate an Initial Service batch\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia.\n\n• Open /admin/compliance (or click the new 📋 Compliance tab in the sidebar).\n\n• In the Generate a new batch form, pick the pilot zone and leave the notice type on Initial Service Notice .\n\n• Optionally type a note (e.g. \"First mailing — Kirk USPS drop\" ).\n\n• Click Generate batch · N letters . The button shows the count based on how many residents the zone currently has.\n\nExpected: A new row appears at the top of Recent batches\nwith the zone name, type, resident count, an amber GENERATED badge,\nand today's date. No letters have been printed or mailed yet — that's still Tia's call.\n\n📩 Initial Service gating (NEW · Apr 27)\n\nInitial Service Notices skip residents whose service has already started — anyone with at\nleast one completed pickup. The form says so, and the generated batch row reads\nsomething like \"12 units · 3 active residents skipped\" . Semi-Annual\nupdates still go to every resident in the zone (no gating).\n\nThe included resident UIDs are snapshotted onto the batch doc ( residentIds\nfield), so reprints later — even after more residents start service — produce the same\nletters. Pre-gating batches without that field fall back to \"all residents in zone.\"\n\nVerify:\n\nThe resident count on the row matches the one the form offered before clicking\n\nThe note (if entered) shows up in the row subtitle\n\nAn empty zone (0 residents) still generates a batch but its print view shows the empty-state\n\nNEW: After processing a bag for one resident in the zone, generating a fresh Initial Service batch shows that resident as \"skipped\" — Semi-Annual still includes them\n\nNEW: Re-printing the original batch still produces letters for the same N residents (snapshot is immutable)\n\nScenario 6 — Print the letters\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia.\n\n• On the batch row, click Print letters . A new tab opens at /admin/compliance/[batchId]/print .\n\n• Scroll through the preview — there should be one letter per resident, with the resident's name, street address, zone pickup day, depot location, and the list of accepted materials.\n\n• Click 🖨️ Print all letters . The browser print dialog opens.\n\n• Choose Save as PDF to verify the output; or send to an actual printer.\n\nExpected: The admin sidebar, header, and the two buttons at the top of\nthe screen all disappear in the print preview. Each resident's letter begins on its own\npage (CSS break-after-page ). Letters are sorted alphabetically by street.\n\nVerify:\n\nOne page per resident — no two letters share a page\n\nThe \"Semi-Annual Update\" copy is different from the \"Initial Service\" copy (test by generating both types and comparing)\n\nResidents without an address on file are silently skipped (pilot choice)\n\nScenario 7 — Mark the batch as mailed\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia.\n\n• Back on /admin/compliance , find the batch.\n\n• Click Mark as mailed .\n\n• The row's badge flips from amber GENERATED to green MAILED , and the subtitle gains a \"Mailed [date]\" note.\n\nExpected: The Mark as mailed button disappears (idempotent —\nno accidental double-mark). In Firestore, the complianceBatches document has\nstatus = \"mailed\" , a mailedAt timestamp, and Tia's uid in\nmailedBy . Print letters is still available for re-prints.\n\nVerify:\n\nClicking Mark-as-mailed twice does nothing (button disappears after the first click)\n\nGreen MAILED badge persists across page reloads\n\nmailedBy is Tia's uid, not the resident's\n\nScenario 8 — Semi-Annual update batch\n\nAdmin Signed in as Tia.\n\n• Generate a second batch for the same zone — this time pick Semi-Annual Update .\n\n• Open its print view.\n\nExpected: The heading on each page reads Semi-Annual Recycling\nService Update (not Notice of New Residential Recycling Service ). The body\ncopy says this is the required semi-annual update and invites contact-info corrections.\nThe service-details table is the same (pickup day, depot, accepted materials,\nholidays).\n\nVerify:\n\nBoth batches coexist — the first stays in the list\n\nEach batch is independently mailable\n\nQuick troubleshooting\n\nWhat you see\nLikely cause\nWhat to try\n\nZone performance table is empty\nNo zones created yet.\nOpen /admin/zones and create at least one.\n\nA zone's bags and weight are 0 but Goode processed bags this week\nThe resident those bags belong to doesn't have the zone assigned.\nOpen /admin/users , find the resident, confirm zoneId matches.\n\nContamination alerts never populate\nEvery processed bag used severity None .\nHave Goode flip a couple of bags to Minor / Major / Severe in Phase 7, then reload the dashboard.\n\nLeaderboard missing the operator I just watched complete pickups\nThose pickup docs don't have an operatorId set, or the completions happened before the 1st of the month.\nCheck pickups in Firestore for a status = completed doc with the expected operatorId . Historical months are intentionally ignored.\n\n\"Generate batch\" form disabled\nThere are no zones yet.\nCreate a zone first in /admin/zones .\n\nPrint view shows \"No residents with addresses in this zone\"\nThe zone has residents but none have a linked addressId (rare once residents finish signup).\nHave the affected resident re-run onboarding, or fix the users.addressId field directly in Firestore for seeded accounts.\n\nPrint output still shows the admin sidebar / nav\nBrowser's print media query isn't applying — check the print dialog's \"Options → Headers and footers\" and \"Background graphics\" switches.\nTurn Background graphics OFF in the print dialog. The layout uses print:hidden which is driven by the media query.\n\nWhat's not testable yet\n\nReal mailing automation (Lob, Click2Mail, etc.) is out of scope for the pilot — Tia prints\nletters and drops them in a USPS box, then comes back here to mark the batch mailed.\nPer-resident opt-out tracking and automatic semi-annual reminders are future work; today\nthe admin just remembers to generate a new Semi-Annual batch every six months. Contamination\nstrikes are read-only — the app does not yet send a warning email or SMS when a resident\ncrosses a threshold; Tia takes that action manually.\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Phase 9 testing guide · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Testing as an Admin (role-admin.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nAdmin Testing as an admin\n\nThe back-office role. You have the most surface area — invites, zones, pricing,\nredemptions, routes, sticker sheets, compliance mailings. Do this in two sessions:\nfoundation setup first, then polish and operations.\n\nDevice: 💻 Laptop exclusively.\n\nWho you'll be: Tia ( tia.admin@wbct-pilot.test ).\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nAs admin you see the back-end of every stub — expect:\n\n• Bag orders — residents never actually pay. Stripe is mocked, so every order lands as paid.\n\n• Gift card redemptions — requests pile into your queue for manual fulfillment. No auto-issue.\n\n• Compliance notices — the app generates a PDF; you print + mail it, then click \"Mark as mailed.\"\n\n• SMS — all outbound texts are written to a Firestore smsLog collection (and the server console), not actually sent.\n\n• Invite emails — not sent. Copy the invite link from the admin UI and forward it yourself.\n\nYour testing path\n\nSession 1 — foundation (~30 min)\n\nEvery other role depends on this session. Run it first, then hand off to residents /\noperator / depot worker before coming back for Session 2.\n\n1\n\nSign in and read the overview dashboard\n\nConfirm KPIs load (may be zero), sidebar is visible, no \"TODO\" placeholders.\n\n→ Admin dashboard · Scenario 1\n\n2\n\nInvite the operator and the depot worker\n\nSend invites. For depot worker, the Depot dropdown must appear (link\nthem to the pilot depot). Confirm the invites show as pending and the links are\ncopyable.\n\n→ Admin dashboard · Scenario 2\n\n3\n\nCreate zones and a depot, configure accepted materials\n\nCheck existing pilot zone, add a test one, edit, delete. Create a depot and tie it to the zone.\n\nApr 27 addition: on each depot card, toggle the\naccepted materials chips so the depot only processes the commodities it\nactually handles. The depot worker's process form will hide the rest.\n\n→ Admin dashboard · Scenarios 4 & 4b\n\n4\n\nSet Yellow Sheet prices, add custom materials, schedule campaigns\n\nWithout prices, the resident calculator and depot processing both show zero points.\n\nApr 27 additions:\nadd new materials (compost / textile / electronics) via the\n+ Add to yellow sheet composer; toggle the Active checkbox to hide a\nretired commodity (soft-delete only). In the new Pricing campaigns section\nbelow the table, schedule a global ×N multiplier for selected materials\nover a date range — Aguirre and Goode both see it live.\n\n→ Admin dashboard · Scenarios 5 / 5b / 5c\n\n5\n\nPre-generate sticker sheets\n\nGenerate a sheet, inspect the print preview, confirm it writes 1 sheet doc + 10 bag docs.\n\n→ QR sticker generation · Scenarios 1–3\n\n6\n\nBuild tomorrow's route\n\nPull pending pickups + deliveries for a zone, optimize stop order, assign the operator, save.\n\n→ Route builder · Scenarios 1–3\n\nHandoff.\nAt this point operators and residents can start testing. Come back to Session 2 once\nthey've generated some data — contamination flags, completed routes, processed bags — so\nthe polish panels have something to display.\n\nSession 2 — polish & operations (~20 min)\n\nRun after the operator + depot testing has generated data. The panels below are\ndata-driven and empty until then.\n\n7\n\nRead the polish panels\n\nZone performance table, contamination alerts (with escalating strike levels), operator\nleaderboard — all should reflect what the other roles just did.\n\n→ Admin polish · Scenarios 1–4\n\n8\n\nGenerate a compliance notice batch\n\nInitial Service Notice or Semi-Annual Update — app generates the PDF, you click \"Mark\nas mailed\" after sending. No postage automation during the pilot.\n\nApr 27 addition: Initial Service batches now\nskip residents whose service has already started (any completed pickup) and the batch\nrow shows a \" N active residents skipped \" count. Semi-Annual still goes to\neveryone in the zone.\n\n→ Admin polish · Scenario 5\n\n9\n\nGift-card redemption queue\n\nApprove a pending resident request. The card is emailed manually — the app just moves\nthe request through states.\n\n→ Admin dashboard · Scenarios 6–7\n\n10\n\nPost-deploy smoke test (optional)\n\nAfter the dev team ships a production deploy, a 5-minute check that every role can\nstill sign in and all the key pages load.\n\n→ Post-deploy smoke test\n\nWhat you're not testing\n\n• Automatic gift-card fulfillment — manual email only.\n\n• Automated compliance mailings — generate + mark-as-mailed only.\n\n• Real email delivery of invites — logged to console during the pilot.\n\n← Previous · Step 4\nAccounts & sign-in\n\nNext · Step 6\nResident flow →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Role guide · Admin · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Testing as a Depot Worker (role-depot.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nDepot Testing as a depot worker\n\nThe sorting-floor role. Receive completed routes, scan bags into the process form, weigh\nthe 7 materials, flag contamination, submit. Each submission awards points to the\nresident and adds to inventory — all in one atomic write.\n\nDevice: 💻 Laptop or tablet — the form has 7 weight fields, so a real\nkeyboard is easier than thumbs-on-glass.\n\nWho you'll be: Depot Goode ( depot.goode@wbct-pilot.test ).\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\n\n• Weighing is manual — weights are typed in by hand. No Bluetooth / USB scale integration in the pilot.\n\n• SMS to residents (points awarded) is logged to the server console, not sent as a real text.\n\nYour testing path\n\n0\n\nPrerequisites\n\nDepot testing needs earlier roles to have done their part — the Incoming list is empty without them.\n\n• Phase 4 — admin has set Yellow Sheet prices for all 7 materials and created a depot linked to Goode.\n\n• Phase 5 — a route has been built for an operator.\n\n• Phase 6 · Scenario 6 — the operator has completed the route and tapped Deliver to depot .\n\n1\n\nSee the incoming route\n\nSign in as Goode. The depot home redirects to /depot/incoming . You\nshould see the operator's completed route as a NEW card.\n\n→ Depot day · Scenarios 1–2\n\n2\n\nScan a bag into the process form\n\nTap the route, then scan a bag QR (or type the printed code). The form opens with the resident's name and bag number.\n\n→ Depot day · Scenario 3\n\n3\n\nProcess a clean bag\n\nType weights in the material rows. Leave contamination on None . Watch the\nlive points preview, then submit. Cross-check that the resident's points balance\nactually went up by the expected amount.\n\nApr 27 additions: the form now only renders\nrows for materials this depot accepts (configured by admin). If admin has a\nPricing campaign live, a 🔥 Active campaign banner appears\nabove contamination and the points preview reflects the multiplier — see\nPhase 7 · Scenario 4b .\n\n→ Depot day · Scenarios 4 & 4b\n\nThe happy path ends here.\nSteps 1–3 above are the 15-minute release gate. Everything below is optional edge-case\ntesting. Stop here unless you're specifically investigating a behavior.\n\n★\n\nAdvanced: contamination, separated bags, double-processing guard, inventory, SMS stub\n\nContamination math (None/Minor/Major/Severe = 0/30/60/90% penalty), separated-bag 2×\nmultiplier, re-processing refusal, running inventory totals, and verifying the SMS\nstub logs correctly.\n\n→ Depot day · Scenarios 5–10 (Advanced section)\n\nWhat you're not testing\n\n• Bluetooth/USB scale integration — weight is manual entry.\n\n• Real SMS receipts — logged to console during the pilot.\n\n• Depot-manager flows (mill shipments, inventory drawdown) — that's Phase 8, not yet built.\n\n← Previous · Step 7\nOperator flow\n\nNext · Step 9\nPost-deploy smoke test →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Role guide · Depot worker · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Testing as an Operator (role-operator.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nOperator Testing as an operator\n\nThe driver's day. Start a route, scan bags at each stop, deliver sticker sheets, close\nout at the depot. ~25 minutes once the prereqs are in place.\n\nDevice: 📱 Phone (primary — the app is designed phone-first). 💻 Laptop on the\nside for Tia, if you need to set up or inspect routes.\n\nWho you'll be: Operator Aguirre ( operator.aguirre@wbct-pilot.test ).\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\n\n• SMS to residents (driver-on-the-way, pickup complete) is logged to the server console, not sent as a real text.\n\n• \"Driver on the way\" is tap-triggered by you, not auto-fired from GPS geofencing.\n\nYour testing path\n\nThe operator flow has prerequisites that must be done by an admin first. Don't skip step 0.\n\n0\n\nPrerequisites (admin must do these first)\n\nUntil Tia has built a route for you, the operator home screen just says \"No active\nroute.\" Ask her (or do it yourself on the admin laptop) to:\n\n• Set Yellow Sheet prices — Phase 4 · Scenario 5.\n\n• Make sure a resident has a scanned bag ready for pickup — Phase 3 · Scenario 4.\n\n• Build today's route and assign it to you — Phase 5 .\n\n1\n\nSign in on a phone\n\nSign in as the operator. You should land at /operator and see today's route.\n\n→ Accounts & sign-in · Scenario 5 (or just use the seeded operator)\n\n2\n\nSee today's route, start it, text \"on the way\"\n\nConfirm you see the correct stops, no one else's. Start the route, then tap the\ndriver-on-the-way button (SMS is stubbed — button just shows a toast).\n\n→ Operator day · Scenarios 1–2\n\n3\n\nComplete a pickup (scan a QR, take a photo)\n\nScan the resident's bag sticker. If the camera misbehaves, use the manual code entry\nfallback. Take any photo for the \"I was here\" record.\n\n→ Operator day · Scenario 3\n\n4\n\nTry Skip vs Not Out vs Issue\n\nThree ways a stop can end badly — confirm each has the expected follow-up (skip = retry\nlater, not-out = marked for next visit, issue = logged for admin).\n\n→ Operator day · Scenario 4\n\n5\n\nDeliver a new sticker sheet\n\nHand off a sheet to a resident with a pending bag order. Claims 10 bags to them atomically.\n\n→ Operator day · Scenario 5\n\n6\n\nClose out the route\n\nTap \"Deliver to depot\" on the summary screen. Once you do, the route shows up under the\ndepot worker's Incoming list — handoff complete.\n\n→ Operator day · Scenario 6\n\nWhen you're done.\nTick every checkbox in the Phase 6 guide and report any\nregressions. The depot worker takes over from here.\n\nWhat you're not testing\n\n• GPS geofencing — driver-on-the-way is a tap, not automatic.\n\n• Real SMS to residents — all stubbed.\n\n← Previous · Step 6\nResident flow\n\nNext · Step 8\nDepot flow →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Role guide · Operator · Pilot April 2026\n\n---\n\n### GUIDE: We Buy Clean Trash — Testing as a Resident (role-resident.html)\n\n← Back to full guide · 🔑 Test users · 📖 Glossary\n\nResident Testing as a resident\n\nThe homeowner path. Sign up, order bags, scan one before pickup, check points, try\nredeeming. ~20 minutes end-to-end with the seeded accounts.\n\nDevice: 📱 Phone (primary). 💻 Laptop works too but the resident app is\ndesigned phone-first.\n\nWho you'll be: Resident Aguirre ( resident.aguirre@example.com ) from the\nTest users card .\n\n⚠️ What's stubbed in this flow\nA few things look real but don't fully fire during the pilot. Please don't file these as bugs:\n\n• Ordering bags — checkout ends on a mock \"paid\" screen. No real card is charged.\n\n• Redeeming a gift card — the request lands in Tia's admin queue; she fulfills it manually, not auto.\n\n• \"Pay trash bill\" and \"Donate\" buttons are disabled on purpose.\n\n• SMS notifications are logged to the server console, not sent as real texts.\n\nYour testing path\n\nWork through the steps below in order. Each step links to the relevant phase guide — treat\nthis page as the map; the phase guides are the detail.\n\n1\n\nSign in (or sign up from scratch)\n\nMake sure sign-in works for your resident account. If you want to also experience the\nsignup flow, create a new resident on top of the seeded ones.\n\n→ Accounts & sign-in · Scenarios 1–3\n\n2\n\nExplore the dashboard\n\nConfirm the points balance, next-pickup card, and bottom nav all load. This is the first\nscreen a real pilot customer will see every day.\n\nApr 27 additions to verify: the\nsignup-bonus modal pops once on first visit, the\nredemption-progress card shows points-to-$10-gift-card, the order CTA reads\n\"Order your first box of bags\" for new accounts, and a\n🔥 Bonus running now banner appears whenever admin has a live pricing campaign.\n\n→ Resident app · Scenario 2\n\n3\n\nOrder a sheet of bags\n\nPlace an order. The checkout is mocked — no real card is charged.\nConfirm the delivery rule toggles at $20, then open the history page to confirm the\norder (and any earlier orders) appear there.\n\n→ Resident app · Scenarios 3 & 3b\n\n4\n\nScan a bag before pickup day\n\nScan one of your sticker QR codes to register the bag as out for pickup. Ask the dev\nteam to pre-issue a sheet if you don't have one yet.\n\n→ Resident app · Scenario 4\n\n5\n\nTry the pre-signup AI scan\n\nOpen an incognito window and tap Try the scan on the landing page. Point the\ncamera at a recyclable. Confirm you get an estimate without needing to sign up.\n\n→ Resident app · Scenario 1\n\n6\n\nCheck the payout calculator + rewards\n\nPlay with the calculator to see how different weights translate to points, then open\nRewards to see your signup bonus ledger entry.\n\nApr 27 additions: each weight input shows a\nlbs suffix; if admin has a campaign live, affected material rows display\na ×N badge and the preview math reflects the multiplier.\n\n→ Resident app · Scenarios 5–7\n\nWhen you're done.\nTick every checkbox in the Phase 3 guide, note anything that\ndidn't look right, and send the list back. You don't need to touch any other phase as a\nresident.\n\nMore resident touches (NEW · Jun 9)\nA few extras worth a glance while you test: a welcome email goes out on\nsignup; a zone can have multiple pickup days (the dashboard shows the next\none for your address); residents get delivery-window notifications when an\norder is on its way; and a refer-a-friend option lets residents share the\napp.\n\nWhat you're not testing\n\n• \"Pay trash bill\" and \"Donate\" redemptions — disabled on purpose during the pilot.\n\n• Real gift-card fulfillment — the request queues for an admin to send manually.\n\n• Real SMS alerts — all messages log to the server console instead.\n\n← Previous · Step 5\nAdmin — your main path\n\nNext · Step 7\nOperator flow →\n\nWe Buy Clean Trash · Role guide · Resident · Pilot April 2026";
